learning_objectives:
Na succesvolle afronding van dit vak ben je in staat tot:

1: Het opstellen van een gedetailleerde planning voor een experiment op basis van een experimentele procedure, inclusief een veiligheidsanalyse en het kiezen van de juiste synthese- of meetopstelling;
2: Het uitvoeren van enkele veelgebruikte experimentele technieken binnen de (an)organische synthese, analytische chemie, biochemie, biotechnologie en chemische technologie;
3: Het toepassen van de noodzakelijke maatregelen om experimenten uit te voeren op een wijze die veilig is voor jezelf, omstanders en het milieu;
4: Het ordenen van observaties en meetresultaten in een labjournaal, tabel of grafiek;
5: Het berekenen van de numerieke meetonzekerheid behorende bij de uitkomst van een experiment;
6: Het analyseren van de resultaten van een experiment in een beknopt meetrapport.

Na het afronden van PBV, ben je in staat om met praktisch werk binnen de onderzoeksgroepen (Leren Onderzoeken, zie 4051LEON1) te starten.

Onderdeel Fysische Chemie

De student:

- Kan rekenen aan chemische evenwichten op basis van entropie en enthalpie;
- Begrijpt de moleculaire achtergrond van entropie en enthalpie en kan op basis hiervan verklaren hoe reactie-omstandigheden als temperatuur, druk en concentratie het evenwicht van reacties beïnvloeden;
- Kent de eigenschappen van een ideale oplossing en kan afwijkingen van het ideaal gedrag onder woorden brengen;
- Kan de thermodynamische eigenschappen van elektrolyt oplossingen onderscheiden en de Debye-Hueckel theorie toepassen;
- Kan met de wet van Nernst werken en de verschillende elektrochemische cellen onderscheiden;
- Begrijpt de moleculaire achtergrond van transport, diffusie en migratie;
- Kent de elementaire aspecten van de kinetiek van elektrode reacties;
- Kan werken met eenvoudige wiskundige modellen voor chemische reacties aan elektrochemische en katalytische oppervlakken in combinatie met massa transport.

Onderdeel Kinetiek

De student kan:
- met MS Excel experimentele gegevens correct weergeven om kinetische parameters te bepalen (zoals orden van reactie, reactiesnelheidsconstanten en activeringsbarriëres);
- op basis van kinetische parameters cruciale aspecten aan reactiemechanismen interpreteren en toelichten;
- een eenvoudig wiskundig model opzetten van een reactiemechanisme, dit oplossen en grafisch weergeven;
- de opbouw van en beweging over potentiaaloppervlakken semi-kwantitatief weergeven.

De student heeft:

- Kennis van een aantal belangrijke katalytische processen zowel homogeen, biokatalytisch, heterogeen als elektrokatalytisch;
- Inzicht in de belangrijkste verschillen, overeenkomsten, voor- en nadelen en toepassingsgebieden van de vier verschillende gebieden van de katalyse;
- Vaardigheid in het toepassen van de principes van chemische reactiekinetiek op katalytische processen en mechanismen;
- Inzicht in de werking, moleculaire mechanismen, preparatie, karakterisering, en stabiliteit van een aantal katalysatoren en katalytische processen in de vier katalysegebieden;
- Inzicht in de toepassing van katalyse in de industrie.

The student:

1. Literature Review/study:
- Is able to study relevant scientific literature and to look up data sources and assess them by value.

2. Preparing/Setting up research:
- Is able to prepare experiments (formulation research question, setting up research plan);
- Environment and safety: can work safely with chemicals, taking into account their physical and chemical properties, including any specific hazards associated with the use of these materials;
- Is skilled in making a risk analysis of the use of chemicals.

3. Collecting/Generating Research data:
- Is able to perform standard laboratory prescriptions;
- May carry out observations and measurements under limited supervision, as well as their correct reporting (including a well-kept lab journal).

4. Analysing and processing of research material:
- Can interpret the observations and data obtained in the laboratory and relate it to existing and/or an appropriate theory;
- Ethics: Has an understanding of his/her professional and ethical responsibility.

5. Working posture and teamwork
- Shows dedication, motivation, enthusiasm, creativity for the thesis work performed;
- Is able to function in a (multicultural and multidisciplinary) team;
- Is able to actively participate, cooperate effectively, effectively discuss.

6. Reporting:
- Is able to report in writing and orally with attention to requirements for scientific communication and accountability;
- Is able to communicate effectively in Dutch and English, using modern presentation techniques.

description:
Het vak Numerieke Technieken is gericht op het gebruik van numerieke technieken voor het oplossen van complexe vraagstukken met behulp van een computer. De cursus heeft een focus op het gebruik van zulke numerieke technieken voor het oplossen van problemen in de chemie en chemical engineering. De volgende onderwerpen worden behandeld:

1. Introductie Python; introductie programmeren en algoritmes;
2. Statistische analyse en plotten van data;
3. Curve fitting en regressie;
4. Numerieke integratie en differentiatie;
5. Oplossen van (stelsels van) lineaire en niet-lineaire vergelijkingen;
6. Numeriek oplossen van gekoppelde differentiaal vergelijkingen;
7. Monte Carlo technieken.

This course is taught at Leiden University.
Lecturer: Prof.dr. A. Achúcarro (achucar@lorentz.leidenuniv.nl)


The classical theory of gravity can be interpreted as the geometry of space-time.It combines the language of field theory with the language of differential geometry on manifolds. The basic concepts of these subjects will be introduced and it will be shown how they are used to describe gravitational phenomena, from relativistic corrections to orbits of planets and stars, to gravitational waves, black holes and the evolution of the universe at large.

Topics:

Equivalence principle, geodesic motion;

Differential geometry on manifolds, including the concepts of metric, connection and curvature;

Symmetries and Killing vectores;

Einstein equations, variational principle;

Schwarzschild solution, stars and black holes;

Cosmology of a homogeneous and isotropic universe;

Gravitational waves.

learning_objectives:
This course provides the basis for work in relativistic astronomy, cosmology, quantum gravity and applications. It will :

acquaint you with the concepts of field theory and differential geometry;

teach you to use these concepts to solve problems of gravitational physics and cosmology;

prepare you for advanced courses on gravitational physics and cosmology;

allow you to access the scientific literature on these topics and understand the recent progress in experimental and observational research.

At the end of this course, the student will be able to:

-Implement strategic thinking for material selection;

-Link the material properties to well-functioning simplified aerospace components;

-Describe and discuss the relationship between the composition and structure of a material and its properties;

-Describe the fundamental reasons behind material behaviour (mechanical, thermal, corrosion, etc.);

-Derive and use material indices and material charts to select materials for a given simple geometry and loading conditions;

-Select materials considering application, environmental resistance, and sustainability and ethical aspects;

-Design simple products considering material properties and loading conditions.

The course aims at providing a learning experience for students, in which they apply the (theoretical) content of thematic courses in a practical, project setting. The students are organized in groups working on real-life problem cases, resulting in a professional output product: a number of work package reports and technical presentations.

At the end of this project-based course, student will be able to

- Apply knowledge and practice the methods from AE courses for the design of (sub)systems;

- Determine and describe design options;

- Evaluate the performance of systems, describe trade-offs and reflect on the selected design choices;

- Use (online) communication tools to effectively co-operate in a team;

- Prepare and give an oral presentation on a technical subject, using effective visual aids.

description:
Students work in groups of ~10 on projects that deal with the topics of test, analysis en simulation. These projects are defined and supervised by academic staff from the Aerospace Faculty of the Delft University of Technology. The work to be carried out by the group consists of at least the following activities:

Analysis of the data; collecting the data is not a part of the project, it will be provided by the tutor of the project group

Verification of data analysis results through comparison with theory, simulations, and literature;

Interpretation of the results, resulting in conclusions with regards to the research question and recommendations;

Documentation of the research question, test, simulation and analysis results, and the conclusions in a scientific report;

Write a self-reflection. The student should reflect both on his/her technical input and functioning in the research group;

Peer review of reports as written by students from other groups.

learning_objectives:
The thematic projects in the Aerospace Engineering Bachelor Program aim to provide learning experiences for students that enable them to integrate the (theoretical) content of thematic courses in a practical, active setting. They aim to simulate a professional environment in which students act within a professional role, on real-life problems which result in a professional output product, such as a technical report, a poster, an abstract, a scientific presentation, or a scientific paper. The projects are mandatory elements of the program. Each semester contains one thematic project. The theme of this 4th semester project is data analysis and evaluation. The outcome of the project work is to be documented in a scientific report. The aim of the project is that at the end of the project, the student will be able:

To analyse an experimental set-up or simulation model and assess its performance with regard to answering research questions;

To carry out an analysis of experimental and/or model results;

To formulate conclusions with the aim to answer a research question;

To write a research report and present the research to others;

To function as a member of a research team in a research environment;

To be able to critically reflect on one's own research as well as others using a peer format.

At the end of the course, students will be able to:

- Describe the most-commonly used Machine Learning models and their scope of applicability;

- Match the appropriate class of models to a given Aerospace Engineering application;

- Understand the range of applicability and the limitations of the models studied;

- Apply the models studied using well-established open-source Python packages;

- Understand and use concepts and jargon borrowed from the Artificial Intelligence community in the context of problems in Aerospace Engineering;

- Apply conventional Artificial Intelligence algorithms to real-life problems in Aerospace Engineering.

description:
Aeroacoustics is one of the most important challenges for engineering students approaching either an industrial or an academic career in propulsion, wind energy and aerodynamics. The aim of the course Fundamentals of Aeroacoustics is to provide the students with a fundamental knowledge to model and predict aerodynamic noise generation and propagation. In particular, students will learn how to:

describe how aerodynamic noise is generated and radiated;

identify the most important sound generation mechanisms in practical situations and select the suitable aeroacoustic model to describe those mechanisms;

setup a numerical simulation to predict the noise generated by elementary unsteady flows.

learning_objectives:
The student has to be able:

to formulate a practical air transportation problem as an agent or a multiagent system model;

to identify appropriate agent-based methods and to apply them;

to implement agent-based models;

to perform agent-based simulation, interpret and analyze simulation results;

to be able to apply agent-based optimization techniques.

description:
Thorough treatment of the fundamentals of astrodynamics; topics are treated in a mathematically cohesive way. You will acquire fundamental insights into the theory and methods of astrodynamics as well as working knowledge to compute and analyze spacecraft trajectories.

Topics include:
• Introduction to astrodynamics;
• two-body and many-body problems;
• relative motion;
• coordinates, reference frames, orbital elements, and time;
• geocentric, cislunar, and interplanetary flight;
• introduction to orbital perturbations.

learning_objectives:
You will be able to:

1. Provide full and accurate descriptions of the fundamental concepts and phenomena of astrodynamics;

2. Explain the relationships between astrodynamics concepts & phenomena and the mathematics & physics theory that describes them;

3. Apply the relevant relationships, models, and methods for astrodynamics analysis;

4. Derive and manipulate the equations of motion for a spacecraft subject to gravitational and other forces;

5. Apply the equations of motion, their solutions, and related equations to analyze the absolute and relative positions and velocities of spacecraft as a function of time.

Additional detail will be available in the course syllabus.

Within the context of the selected astrodynamics topics:

Extend your insight and analytical abilities beyond the Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and related coursework;

Actively apply your knowledge and skills accumulated through Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and related coursework;

Tackle "open-ended assignments with a hands-on, scientific approach as a first foray into astrodynamics research.

At the end of this course, students should be able to:

explain the specifics, potentials, challenges and differences among various types of attitude and orbit control systems;

design a credible functional and physical architecture of an ADCS/GNC system with details, for a specified application related to multi-satellite systems;

assess the AOCS/GNC performance.

At the end of this course, you should be able to:

- Describe different maintenance strategies and select the appropriate one for a given problem, taking into account different aspects (e.g. technical, regulations, planning);

- Describe the working principles of different non-destructive inspection techniques, their pros and cons and their applicability to different materials;

- Explain the relationship between certification and maintenance requirements and (documentation of) procedures;

- Develop a maintenance programme for a simple case;

- Select an appropriate design philosophy to ensure structural integrity of a given part throughout the aircraft lifetime.

By the end of this course, you will be able to:

explain different sources of nonlinear behavior and their significance for the stability and responses of aeroelastic systems;

apply nonlinear system analysis methodologies to nonlinear aeroelastic systems;

understand, derive, and apply flexible aircraft flight dynamics;

understand the difference between different panel methods for linear aerodynamics;

apply panel methods to the analysis of aeroelastic lifting surfaces.

Please find below the learning objectives of the internship course AE5051.

By the end of the internship:

1. You can apply engineering knowledge and (technical) skills in a professional setting in an analytical academic way;

2. You can demonstrate this knowledge and these skills in a technical report;

3. You are able to demonstrate competences and (collaboration) skills in a professional environment;

4. You can write a self-reflection report about your personal functioning and the progress/improvement(s) that you made during your internship.

