have defined the project goal and plan;

have made a realistic time planning;

know the pitfalls that can result in delays in the project and how to deal with these;

have an overview of the relevant literature in the field of your project;

have written a report (in English) described in the course content.

The goals of the internship are:

To become familiar with a professional working environment for a physicist;

To use academic knowledge and skills, acquired in the degree course, to solve problems or be active in design related to Applied Physics, in a professional working environment.

Skills should be acquired on the following topics:
1. Performance: Productivity, Independence, Practical skills, Theoretical knowledge, Creative thinking, Time management;
2. Communication: Reporting, Presentation, Interpersonal;
3. Competences: Motivation en enthusiasm, Critical attitude, Taking initiative.

At the end of the project, the student should be able to:

read and describe the international research literature;

plan a research project and meet deadlines;

work independently on an academic level in a R&D environment;

work in a (possibly interdisciplinary) and multicultural team of experts and supporting technicians;

present their results in English by means of a presentation to an expert audience and a scientific report.

After the project the student is able to fulfil the following requirements:

- use online data sources, read and understand international research literature;

- plan a research project and meet deadlines;

- preparation and design of experiments or simulations;

- perform experiments or simulations;

- analyse data or results;

- work in a (possibly interdisciplinary and) multicultural team of experts and supporting technicians;

- present results in English by means of a presentation to an expert audience and a scientific report.

After completing the course, students can:

- Explain various forms of (long-term) neighbourhood change and their implications for neighbourhoods and individual residents, both from a theoretical and empirical perspective;

- Identify the underlying mechanisms in urban policies and urban design which aim to create positive benefits from social inequality and diversity, or combat their perceived negative implications;

- Evaluate the socio-spatial implications of various forms of social inequality and related policies from a multi-actor perspective;

- Design strategies (in physical, social, cultural and economic sense) for diversity-related problems in neighbourhoods, in collaboration with relevant stakeholders, taking into account their roles and resources.

By the end of this course students will be able to:

Identify elements of the correlation between environmental design and human behaviour from a cross-cultural perspective;

Analyse and synthesise the relations between humans, non-humans and the environment using a combination of architectural and ethnographic methods;

Elaborate research outputs with a synthesis of the field work, literature review and data analysis, using adequate written and visual media;

Use story-telling as a medium to elaborate multi-sensory narratives based on the results of the fieldwork, data collection and analysis;

Elaborate a critical synthesis of the research outputs, using architectural drawings as a preferred medium to enable meaningful communication between different audiences.

Formulate a critical reflection on the research methods, analytical process and criteria for the preparation of the research outputs, acknowledging the researchers' positionality.

description:
This course advances critical tools for the analysis and projection of affordable collective housing design, examining the relevance and applicability of intersectionality theory to housing. The course looks into tendencies of modernist housing production, with a focus on Dutch traditions. Design analyses of case studies are combined with archival studies and reading literature, followed by the development of a critical intersectional archive, organised in the form of a collectively-curated exhibition. AR technology is used to develop an interactive component to assess the project's histories and qualities

The course starts with an introduction to the Dutch national collection of architecture and urban planning at the Nieuwe Instituut in Rotterdam, and a discussion of intersectionality theory including its relevance and applicability to housing design. Students will then develop analyses of selected case studies, revealing the historical and environmental circumstances of the project in relation to its cultural, material and architectural features as follows:

- Students will critically reflect on the socio-economic, political, urban and territorial contexts from which each of these projects emerged, including institutional and governance frameworks and policy-making processes;

- They will investigate how each case study and its designers are embedded in the field of housing histories and cultural production, and how this relates to particular design features (typo-morphology, composition, structure, details, materialisation, technology);

- Students will focus on how a specific form, discourse and urban situation allow for care work, maintenance and other types of labour, and how they promote or challenge notions of gender roles, from the projects conception to its afterlife.

In the last phase of the course, students will develop a critical intersectional archive, which will be organised in the form of a collectively-curated exhibition at the Faculty of Architecture, featuring the analysed case studies through research-based documentation, including graphic and textual outputs, and interactive AR technology.

learning_objectives:
Upon completion of the course the student is able to:

Analyse and synthesise the main generative design components of a housing project, as well as the societal factors that impact it and vice-versa, by using adequate textual and visual outputs;

Understand the concept of intersectionality and its relation with the architecture of housing;

Assess the influence of gender, socio-racial, economic and environmental factors in the development of an affordable collective housing project in relation to its particular circumstance, using the notion of global intersectionality as a critical lens;

Compose and present critical reflection in the form of an exhibition design using adequate academic protocols and archival research;

Apply the results of an analytical study to develop a collaborative curatorial project for a research-based exhibition on affordable collective housing design;

Present a curatorial design strategy to an audience of experts and non-experts;

Work with AR digital technologies as a tool for visualising architectural qualities and histories.

description:
The Analytical Models course aims at furthering the understanding of architectural composition and perception, through thematic, analytical study, making active use of design visualisation and model-making techniques.

This precedent-based designerly study initiative addresses issues of the architectural composition and perception.

Aspects of study include:

Development of study approaches for the benefit of precedent-based composition analysis;

Explorative visualisation of architectural concepts and identification of design phenomena;

Design-driven enquiry making use of various digital modelling applications;

Design-driven enquiry making use of physical scale modelling and graphic representation.

The course can be considered as an interactive learning environment and laboratory for thematic formal study. The exercise brings together ambitions of composition research and evocative, analytical modelling with the didactic opportunities of tangible, physical modelling. The aim is to stimulate the participating students to develop meaningful insights and knowledge on the level of architectural designing and to develop professional skills in the field of design visualisation and communication.

learning_objectives:
After successfully completing this course the student is able to:

- design and engineer a safe and feasible structure on a basic (MSc2) level;

- apply non ordinary materials, like glass, within a loadbearing structure;

- solve practical challenges related to the engineering and construction of a glass structure.

At the end of the course, the student :

has appropriate knowledge of the history of building materials and construction techniques and is able to identify them correctly in a building;

is able to formulate hypotheses on the damage processes, suggest appropriate investigation methods to come to a diagnosis and understand the outcomes of research;

is capable to advice on technical aspects of conservation and rehabilitation interventions of buildings taking the historic values and the future use into account.

is able to document study results visually, in written text and verbally using appropriate technical language

By the end of this course, you should be able to:

- enlarge the disciplinary repertoire used for the investigation, the visualisation and the understanding of topography and for the clarification of spatial identity of a specific landscape;

- understand, internalize and apply the potential interaction between landscape architecture tools, other design disciplines and other fields of science;

- develop a concrete landscape architectural proposal for a specific site;

- elaborate a design proposal in terms of engineering, construction and maintenance.

After the completion of this course you will be able to:

Critically understand the current state-of-the-art, the potential and limits of Computational Intelligence for architectural and engineering design;

Understand the theory of and apply basic Computational Intelligence methods, techniques and tools;

Create a concept design by applying Computational Intelligence methods, techniques and tools, especially towards multi-disciplinary integration.

Upon completion of this course, students

- have gained an overview and understanding of fundamental concepts used within recent architecture-theoretical (and attendant) discourses;

- can articulate both general relationships and differences between these notions and frameworks, and position more specific understandings of singular notions discussed;

- can appropriately apply these notions in a nuanced and layered ways to current research and design questions;

- will have developed academic writing and research skills

At the end of the course a student:

1. has an advanced knowledge of key modes of representation of infrastructure in art, design, and engineering and can reflect on these in discussions, drawings and writings;

2. can reverse-engineer by means of drawing and modelling particular infrastructural artefacts;

3. is capable to interpret and reflect on non-standard cases from practice from a theoretic and design point of view;

4. can reflect on the historical and conceptual relationship between architecture and infrastructure.

Upon successful completion of the course, students will be able to:

- develop a critical understanding of the relationship between adaptability, reuse, and user well-being in contemporary architecture;

- learn to evaluate the potential of occupant data to inform adaptive design strategies;

- explore the aesthetic and functional implications of transforming existing buildings while balancing old and new;

- gain hands-on experience applying reuse strategies to public buildings, resulting in proposals for healthy and inclusive living environments.

By completing the course, the students will be able:

to situate the emergence of new spatial types in relation to the current phase of capitalist development;

to understand the role of these spatial byproducts in the making of the built environment and their inner logic;

to employ different research methods in order to develop a meaningful architectural design proposal;

to develop design strategies aimed to envision public, inclusive and diverse buildings, which can act as urban catalysts;

to experiment with design techniques such as density, hybridization, assemblage, collision.

After this course students will be able to:

- recall key moments and themes in the history and theory of architecture in the context of political, economic, societal and global change;

- analyze and discuss historiographical texts and presentations

- develop a critical understanding of architect's practice and tools through time and space;

- pursue research on historical practices and buildings discussed in class, or, identifies historical examples not presented in the course;

- evaluate existing research in the history and historiography of architecture;

- formulate a research question and first initial idea on a personal research topic for the thesis.

Upon completion of the course the student is able to:

Define, recognize and implement the Fundamentals of Housing Design in analysis and design exercises of residential buildings;

Formulate, explain and argue analytical and design decisions in a coherent and engaging way;

Discuss, organize and demonstrate a collaborative attitude in groups of different size;

Identify, examine and evaluate design choices, experiences, processes and products of both individual, collaborative and external sources in the realm of residential building design;

Organize, develop and design (a) large-scale, multifunctional/residential building(-s) within a contemporary urban context that meets the required standards of the MSc1 level.

Upon completion of the Master 1 design project the student is able to:

- formulate design starting points based on knowledge of the context, the monument and its components and their significance in a broader context of architecture and society;

- create an intervention strategy, explore and select scenario's combining contextual, architectural, ecological, technical qualities, cultural values and program of requirements, referring to a wider discourse;

- create an elaborated and innovative design that shows coherence, significance and correctness in urban, ecological, spatial, functional and material aspects and is related to the formulated starting points;

- explain and present the design, graphically and orally, clearly as a result of study, reflect on the students position in designing with heritage and relate the design proposal to a wider discourse.

Upon completion of the MSc1 design project, you will be able to:

survey a site and its urban setting, and develop a precise spatial understanding of its built fabric through close observation and physical representation;

formulate a spatial proposition for (the transformation of) a medium-sized, semi-public building and develop it into a coherent, layered and technically integrated design, with particular attention to its façade and principal interiors as spaces;

use the large-scale physical model as a means of enquiry, invention and communication as part of an evolving design process

develop and articulate a design argument and a personal position through an iterative process of making, reflection and exchange

Upon completion of the MSc 1 Studio, the students will be able to:

present a coherent, significant, elaborated, correct, and innovative design – on mainline and on aspects for a medium-sized building on MSC 1 level;

develop a design position that considers specific urban conditions and reflects on a coherent design strategy;

relate the assigned functional program to a coherent architectural concept and construction as well;

grasp the complex and heterogeneous potential of the public realm through multiplicity, density, and hybridization;

introduce properties that make architectural interventions less singular in function but more productive, transformative, and resilient;

ensure a design outcome where aesthetic and technical aspects are explicitly integrated and mutually related;

design an innovative architecture that can improve adaptability to climate change and productivity of technical solutions, materials, and building physics;

represent spatial qualities that connect people, architectures, technologies, and materials.

The student will be able to:

- describe and explain the design performance indicators for sustainable, healthy, and comfortable buildings and urban environments;

- explain the various types of energy systems at building, neighbourhood and city level including their effects on a sustainable development;

- optimise the energy performance and Indoor Environmental Quality (IEQ) of buildings and building designs;

- make a conceptual design of HVAC systems in order op optimise energy and IEQ performances;

accounting for the interactions and relations between occupant, indoor environment, energy systems and building.

After successfully completing this course the student should be able to:

- explain scientific integrity and outline ethical issues when doing research;

- execute and report on a technical-scientific research framework for experimental research, simulations, surveys, case studies and research by design;

- analyse a selected topic, in a consistent, coherent and well-structured review paper, with proper referencing;

- interpret the complex relationship between research and design as two aspects within building technology.

By the end of the course, you should be able to:

- identify landscape qualities by making use of phenomenological analysis (human perception);

- design a landscape architectural composition and a planting scheme, which is the expression of the sensory qualities of the landscape (scale, materiality, movement) and landscape processes (relationships between environmental factors and resulting vegetation over time);

- develop a coherent and clear relationship between concept, composition and detail;

- demonstrate a variety of presentation and communication techniques, expressing human experiences (scale, materiality, time, movement);

- reflect on your design, involving ethical considerations as an expression of theoretical perspectives on landscape, landscape architecture and the relation between humans and nature.

After this course, you should be able to:

identify and interpret the historical compositional repertoire of landscape architecture and its role in current practice.

identify, interpret and discuss the formal qualities of woody plants and their spatial and experiential characteristics;

demonstrate a working knowledge of environmental behaviour theories;

describe, interpret, evaluate and critique landscape design in relation to composition and perception of landscape architectural space;

demonstrate analytical drawing and writing techniques that reveal spatial and experiential qualities of planting compositions in relation to their landscape context.

By the end of this course, the student should be able to:

- understand and interpret the Dutch polder landscape, its water management, physical physical patterns, and spatial formation;

- understand the interdependence of hydrological systems, soil conditions, land use and vegetation characteristics;

- apply landscape architectural design tools through scales: from ditch to horizon by employing options and alternatives;

- understand and integrate landscape engineering techniques and ecological knowledge related to plant succession, water/land gradients and maintenance in the design;

- create self-explanatory drawings expressing systems, scale ranges, texture, and materials of an integrative rural landscape proposal.

By the end of this course, the student should be able to:

identify and apply different perspectives on the interaction of natural and cultural processes and how they are expressed in rural landscapes;

demonstrate a working knowledge of historical, social, ecological, and technical interpretative models of landscape;

identify, valuate and represent landscape dynamics, conditions and logics, and their spatial manifestation in relation to land use;

identify and criticise the landscape architectural contribution to rural land reclamation and civil engineering of land and water;

describe and visualise the narrative of a specific landscape as a continuous process in time.

After successfully completing the course, the student

1. is able to identify and compose research design components in the field of MBE;

2. is able to explain the concept of methodological fit;

3. is able to compose a basic mixed method research design in the field of MBE;

4. is able to identify typical ethical issues in research and plan a proper response; and

5. is able to plan research data management in terms of organization, storage, preservation, and sharing of collected data.

At the end of this course students are able to:

relate real estate market characteristics to observed market dynamics;

apply the basic principles of urban economics in real estate market analysis;

assess the financial and economic impact of real estate development following from investment analysis;

apply basic formulas and procedures for converting future cash flows and risk patterns to present value;

evaluate the financial feasibility of (public and private) real estate investment decisions;

debate the role of finance (and in particular financialisation) in real estate and the built environment.

Upon completion of the Design Studio, the students will be able to:

present and discuss a coherent, significant, elaborated, correct and innovative design proposal in general, and on aspects relevant to the MSc2 level;

identify processes of adaptation and transformation of the given urban conditions by constantly relating the human aspects of the changing society to the urban effects of their actions;

represent space in its complex interpenetration of people, architectures, technologies, materials;

express questions of flexibility, adaptation and hybridization when developing a comprehensive design proposal;

address the qualitative aspects of multiplicity to establish a rich layered, inclusive architectural proposal that represents the diversity in society and design;

design an innovative architecture that supports the adaptability to climate change, as well as to the productivity of technical solutions, materials and building physics.

Upon completion of MSc2 Complex Projects design and research studio, the student is able:

- to convincingly present and discuss a coherent, significant, elaborated, correct and innovative design proposal on mainline, and on aspects relevant to the MSC2 level.

- to position the project within a particular theoretical, historical, social or contextual framework;

- to understand the fundamental design process with regard to architectural theory, art, technology and human sciences;

- to demonstrate sufficient insight in and knowledge of the design process;

- to develop critical thinking and reflecting upon the relationships between analysis, conceptualization, method and composition of a design proposal;

- to develop technical skills regarding the architectural drawing on different scales;

- to develop argumentation and graphic skills aiming to consolidate and strongly communicate a design narrative.

Within / Upon completion of the MSc2 studio the students are able to:

- Recognise critical and speculative design approaches from/within other related fields;

- Use and develop experimental methods of investigation and synthesis;

- Define critical design position within the theme of the studio;

- Integrate relevant theoretical knowledge and practical skills into the design process;

- Reflect on the cross-disciplinary role of architecture within the wider discourse of the design field;

- Communicate and defend the architectural project through investigative and critical methods.

Upon completion of the Design Studio, the students will be able to:

convincingly present and discuss a coherent, significant, elaborated, correct and innovative design proposal in general, and on aspects relevant to the MSc2 level;

perform critical comparative research that results in a clearly formulated design hypothesis;

demonstrate how urgent societal issues are addressed in the design project;

demonstrate the necessary argumentation skills to explain and reflect upon the relationships between analysis, conceptualization, method and composition of a design proposal;

represent space in its complex interpenetration of people, architectures, technologies, materials;

confront questions of flexibility, growth and hybridization when developing a comprehensive design proposal.

By the end of this course, you should be able to:

- identify and interpret an urban territory as an earth-life landscape system and as a social-spatial environment, using established analytical methods;

- discover and define (diagnose) a landscape problematique for a mid-sized urban territory with respect to biodiversity, social inclusion and climate adaptation;

- formulate and develop a landscape architectural assignment in an urban context referring to urban ecology and urban forestry perspectives;

- design on various levels of scale and complexity, relating the whole and the component parts in a mutual and coherent relationship;

- resolve and elaborate an urban landscape design (intervention) in spatial, material and technical terms, involving water management, soil mechanics, pavement, planting and micro-climate aspects.

By the end of this course, you should be able to:

- identify and elaborate on theories, concepts, and approaches to urban landscapes and urban landscape design;

- identify and elaborate on spatial design and its social and ecological influences and ethical implications in the context of urban landscape;

- position landscape architecture concerning other spatial design disciplines like visual arts, architecture, civil engineering, ecology, and urbanism;

- logically and critically reflect on a selected discourse verbally, interactively and in writing, based on main academic literature, applying the basic rules of academic writing.

Upon completion of the course, students will:

Have gained experience in design research;

Have deepened their understanding about the entanglements between architecture and societal/social questions;

Be able to relate architecture to historical frameworks;

Communicate design through an alternative, narrative method – the graphic novel short story – that presents the design in all its aspects: the architectural composition, materialization, and (to a lesser extent) the integration of construction.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

1. Apply contextual analysis to create a design hypothesis for a housing project, with a focus on inclusion and the ecological footprint of its residents , culminating in a comprehensive project;

2. Perform a typological analysis of housing design, integrating this research to inform the architectural and technical development of the project.

3. Comprehend the reciprocal relationship between research and design in relation to architectural history and theory, art, technology, human sciences and environmental aspects;

4. Design and present a coherent, significant and innovative design for a mid to large size residential complex (which responds to cultural, functional, structural, regulatory and aesthetic demands

5. Contribute to the added value of the city through a housing program and design project that facilitates the co-existence of fundamental housing values such as: social inclusion, gender equity, affordability, material balance and sustainability thinking;

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

1. perform academic research (formulating useful research questions, acquiring and implementing adequate research techniques and using astute modes of reporting;

2. understand different and changing perspectives on health & care, with range of typologies and demands for healthcare buildings generally and specifically;

3. establish a reciprocal relationship between research and design; use the tools of academic research for the design process; understand diverse needs and wishes of the people in a chosen neighborhood and translate them into spatial demands for the design;

4. reflect on the architectural profession, academic research, personal education, and ethical responsibilities in both architecture and academia. This enables them to position themselves and their design project specifically within the field of health and care design;

5. design a mid to large size residential or mixed complex building or compound, which responds to functional, cultural, structural, regulatory and aesthetic demands en present this publicly.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

1.     conduct architectural research through mapping to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

2.     formulate an individual design position addressing problems and/or challenges relevant to the architectural discipline and in particular to sustainable challenges;

3.     utilise their research findings and apply adequate design methods to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects; 

4.     situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

5.     communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

conduct academic research (literature, archive, value assessment, case studies) to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

formulate an individual design position addressing problems and/or challenges relevant to reuse of military heritage of the Cold War period ;

utilise their research findings and adaptive reuse & research-by-design design methods to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

apply research methods to identify heritage significance, analyse and evaluate the research results, and develop it into design strategies;

develop, compare and select appropriate and innovative design interventions for preserving and improving buildings;

evaluate the applied research methods and assess the impact of the design proposal in relation to the research results;

demonstrate cooperative attitude, academic writing skills, and ability to explore and develop presentation tools that clearly and beautifully express the design interventions;

understand the current challenges of preserving tangible and intangible resources, reducing waste and emissions and argue the research and design decisions in these contexts.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

conduct research in urban and architectural history, relevant theory, cultural practices, human sciences and technology to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief; 

formulate an individual design position addressing challenges relevant to buildings and interiors that accommodate different scales and gradations of public life within the city, and ways in which people within or affected by the scenes made by architecture can be situated in relation to place, time and social and material culture; 

utilise their research findings and design methods (a.o. model making) to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

1. Conduct research by design to systematically analyze the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

2. Formulate an individual position addressing problems and challenges relevant to the design of a hybrid public building, complex or space - on main issues and on aspects to a level fit for the architectural practice;

3. Utilize research-by-design to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

4. Situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

5. Communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

conduct hands-on research in post war urban history and technology, understanding the principles and culture of making of wood/bio-based construction through its physical properties, to systematically analyse and partially rebuild timber buildings, analysing the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief; 

formulate an individual design position addressing problems and/or challenges relevant to the densification of a post war neighbourhood by topping up existing buildings through wood/bio-based construction;

utilise their research findings and hands-on design methods, by extensive model making and drawing through different scales, to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, environmental, situational, functional, spatial, structural, physical and aesthetic aspects;

situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse on urban densification through bio-based architecture and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, the student:

1. is capable of delivering innovative contributions toward the development of sustainable structural, façade, product, climate and computational design, and to technical-scientific research in these areas;

2. has insight in the profession and competences of structural, façade, product, climate or computational designers and their societal and professional role and ethical responsibilities in sustainable development of the built environment;

3. is able to present process and result in a clear and systematic way, reflect on process and work, and argument in a scientific way.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

1.     conduct architectural research through mapping to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

2.     formulate an individual design position addressing problems and/or challenges relevant to the architectural discipline and in particular ‘fragile territories’;

3.     utilise research findings and identify and apply adequate research and design methods to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects; 

4.     situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

5.     communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

Position their design approach addressing relevant issues in relation to the multi-disciplinary propositions of the studio and identify adequate research methods;

Conduct and present in-depth multi-disciplinary research and analysis of the context, design brief and spatial program with adequate methods;

Utilize acquired research findings and develop a design proposal integrating aesthetic, technical and functional requirements of the proposed program;

Communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to a broad audience;

Reflect on the design and building practices, methods and technologies in a broader social, environmental and cultural context.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

conduct architecture research to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

formulate an individual design position addressing challenges relevant to contemporary architecture discipline;

utilise their research findings and organise design methods to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

Formulate their design position addressing pressing issues (social, environmental, cultural…) relevant to the studio brief and identify adequate research methods;

Conduct and present in-depth crossdisciplinary research and analysis of the context, design brief and spatial program with adequate methods;

Utilise research findings and develop their design proposal integrating aesthetic, technical and functional requirements of the proposed spatial program;

Communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to a broad audience;

Reflect on the design and building practices, methods and technologies in a broader social, environmental and cultural context.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

conduct qualitative and quantitative research methods. To systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

formulate an individual design position addressing problems and/or challenges relevant to the studio brief of the DDS graduation studio

utilise their research findings and data informed design methods to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

conduct architectural research to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

formulate an individual design position addressing problems and/or challenges relevant to the extreme environment of the assignment;

utilise their research findings and apply adequate design methods to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

situate your architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

conduct architectural research to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities to develop an individual design brief;

formulate an individual design position addressing problems and/or challenges relevant to extra-/ terrestrial architecture;

utilise their research findings and computational design to robotic construction and operation methods to develop an elaborated architectural design project, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and environmental challenges and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

formulate an articulate design position and develop an individual design assignment relevant to the architectural discipline;

identify and conduct adequate research methods to systematically analyse the societal and physical context, the site, the functional and spatial requirements, and the technical possibilities in order to correctly inform the design brief and the design proposal;

utilize research findings and design methods with a verifiable approach, to develop an elaborated architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, situational, functional, spatial, constructive, structural and aesthetic aspects;

communicate and defend complex design ideas through verbal, visual and written media to different audiences;

situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and in relation to societal and sustainable challenges and reflect on its professional implications.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

1. Analyze the natural and cultural context determined by the studio, through the systematic examination and evaluation of selected aspects of that context, as means to determine a design assignment;

2. Formulate an individual design assignment based on analytical findings, with a clear focus on scale;

3. Design a comprehensive architectural project, using contextual and disciplinary analyses to inform design decisions, and using diverse architectural tools (including drawing, model making and writing) to coherently integrate the formal, technical, performative, and communicative aspects of architecture through heuristic design methods; and

4. Communicate their architectural project to different audiences in verbal, visual, and written media, with particular attention to its inscription within architectural discourse and its professional implications.

Upon completion of this course, the students will be able to:

1.     conduct an exploratory research and show curiosity by employing different experimental methods to analyse the social and physical conditions of an urban site, and the functional, spatial, technical and ecological possibilities;

2.     formulate a relevant and context-sensitive design position to strengthen existing assets, considering the relation between buildings, spaces and societal needs, including aspects and develop an appropriate programme in response to the urban context; 

3.     utilise their research findings to develop an aesthetically compelling and courageous architectural design proposal, coherently integrating contextual, societal,  functional, spatial, material, structural, climatological and ecological aspects;

4.     situate their architectural design proposal in the current architectural discourse and at the intersections between art, historical, anthropological, ecological and technological discourses and reflect on its professional and ethical implications;

5.     collaborate with tutors and peers and communicate complex research and design ideas clearly and persuasively through structured, comprehensible and substantiated verbal, visual and written media to different audiences.

After successfully completing this course, the student is able to:

- recognize important aspects of the institutional framework of the construction process and recognize important aspects of the governance modes that develop within the institutional framework;

- recognize relevant legal aspects (within different phases) of the construction process and analyze these in the context of public and private institutional frameworks;

- estimate, analyse the weight and complexity of the aspects mentioned above and to find the best possible solutions;

- develop ideas to deal with these aspects and provide solutions for problems or conflicts resulting from these aspects in accordance with the fundamental positions of the public and private sectors;

Upon completion of the course, the student will:

have obtained knowledge on practitioners in the fields of architecture and urbanism that work cross-culturally;

have an in-depth understanding of the methods, tools and approaches that are employed in contemporary cross-cultural design practice;

understand how political, cultural and economical factors influence analytical and projective approaches of the built environment.

After this course the student is able:

- to explain the relevance of applied ethics in relation to the design and governance of the data driven city in general;

- to identify concrete ethical issues within the field of data processing in a concrete situation;

- to identify and apply the relevant ethical principles for this concrete situation;

- to create a framework for the application of technology in the data-driven city and to formulate a substantiated standpoint regarding the admissibility of this technology.

By the end of this course, you should be able to:

- systematically identify, visualize and value the main characteristics of the (cultural) landscape design project as a continuous process in time;

- debate and apply methods and tools on landscape transformation, place-making and heritage management;

- use methods and tools to create a visionary transformation plan and present it to a larger audience in different visualization methods;

- understand the processes of creating plans and the role of spatial designers in citizen engagement, facilitating dialogue in participation processes;

- analyse and communicate effectively on complex design (transformation) ideas within a multidisciplinary setting with and for local stakeholders.

Upon completion of the course, the student will be able to:

- assess lighting properties and the impact of architectural lighting design on human perception, physiology and psychology using interdisciplinary theoretical frameworks (FAL1);

- compose an integrated lighting plan (electric light + daylight) for a small/medium sized space using architectural sketching and design tools (FAL2);

- communicate effectively a lighting plan intention and innovation to lighting manufacturers, to designer teams and to clients (FAL3);

- evaluate a lighting plan performance through the lenses of “people, planet, prosperity” sustainable development goals (FAL4).

The work done, and results delivered demonstrate that as a student, you are able to:

- Systematically analyse the company's problem statement, based on initially (possibly unclear) goals and deliverables;

- Apply academic qualities (theorizing, argumentation and reasoning) to proposing solutions to a real company problem;

- Constructively and collaboratively discuss your work within a multidisciplinary team (with people from different educational and cultural backgrounds)

- Systematically evaluate the possible company problem solutions to select the best product / advice with regard to the problem statement;

- Creatively and credibly assemble arguments to design a project advice report / new product recommendations;

- Write a professional joint project report for a company with a multidisciplinary team of students;

- Presenting the results of your project to a professional audience.

At the end of Q1 you will:

- have acquired knowledge, insights and skills in the domains of architectural and interior design, analysis, and presentation;

- have the skill to establish the connection between idea, function and material elaboration of a small scale design;

- have knowledge of different cultural and social conditions, and awareness of their implication and effects on architecture and the interior;

- be able to develop a consistent architectonic / interior design. This covers the materialization of the overall space as well as the detailing

- have developed abilities in the targeted analysis of design precedents and the effective representation and communication of findings, using various representation techniques as well as digital and physical models.

At the end of Q2 you will:

- have the skill to establish the connection between idea, function and material elaboration of a fragment of the building / interior;

- have acquired knowledge, insights and skills in the domains of furniture / interior design, analysis and presentation;

- have developed abilities in the targeted analysis of design precedents and the effective representation and communication of findings, using various representation techniques as well as digital and physical models;

- be able to develop a consistent building/interior fragment. This covers the materialization of the object itself as well as the detailed scale; the processing of technical and aesthetic aspects plays an essential part in this can determine the quality of the perception of the interior by the use of artificial light.

- Has gained knowledge about the cultural and technical aspects of specific themes (for example: user research, materialization and detail)

- Has learned to translate fresh knowledge into a design by doing a short practical study.

- Is able to connect aspects like location, program, target group, functionality and temporariness to the atmospheric quality of building and space;

You will learn about the concept of cultural heritage, and the interaction between history and design. At the end of the course you will be able to:

Explain different concepts and scale levels of heritage and preservation, from academic research to architectural practice;

Explain key terminologies in heritage conservation: values, attributes, authentic­ity, integrity, reversibility etc;

Summarize the main idea of doctrinal texts of conservation: The Venice Charter, the Burra Charter, the Nara Document on Authenticity, the Faro Convention, etc;

Explain common approaches and work methods concerning appreciation, re-purpose and redevelopment of historically valuable objects;

Analyze how diverse cultural traditions, socio-political contexts, and value sys­tems influence the history and practice of heritage conservation in specific cas­es;

Critically evaluate design interventions of heritage sites, articulating and defend­ing your perspectives in discussions on conservation issues.

You will practice the interaction between history and design. At the end of the minor you will have enhanced/improved your:

- knowledge about approaches and work methods concerning appreciation, repurpose and redevelopment of historically valuable objects within an old town;

- ability to analyse complex heritage buildings and ensembles with regards to the urban context and its historic development, the functionality, the materials used and the technical development;

- understand and deal with complexity of the monument;

- ability to make architectural and spatial design choices on different scales, based on an analysis and evaluation of current historic information;

- ability to put historic research and contextual analysis in functional relation with a design task;

- ability to justify your point of view in discussions about the valuation of heritage on different scales and about the merging of old and new in case of an intervention.

You will practice the interaction between history and design. At the end of this course you will have enhanced/improved your:

- knowledge about cultural heritage in the form of urban projects and structures;

- knowledge about relevant processes in urban history and development;

- to use professional terminology;

- knowledge about approaches and work methods concerning appreciation, repurpose and redevelopment of historically valuable objects within an old town;

- ability to analyse heritage buildings and ensembles with regards to the urban context and its historic development, the functionality, the materials used and the technical development;

- to apply different tools of spatial analysis and ability to explain your choice of them;

- ability to put historic research and contextual analysis in functional relation with a design task;

- ability to justify your point of view in discussions about the valuation of heritage on different scales.

After this course, you will be able to:

LO1: Define the fundamental principles and key concepts that contribute to the development of a healthy and inclusive city;

LO2: Discuss the meaning of these concepts in the context of their theoretical background and the broader societal context;

LO3: Identify how socio-spatial and health inequalities manifest themselves on the level of cities and neighbourhoods and provide examples of this manifestation;

LO4: Synthesize the relevant literature for a chosen theme or concept and analyse its spatial implications.

description:
This course is part of the minor "Sustainable Urbanism: the Green-Blue City".

Course overview

The programme consists of two courses of a total of 15 ECs.

- Systems in the City: Sustainable Urban Design (studio) (BK7575): 10 ECs

- Basic Techniques for Sustainable Urban Design (BK7215): 5 ECs

An important task of urban planners and designers is to introduce and design sustainable urban interventions, creating future proof neighbourhoods within the framework of a green blue city. In the Sustainable Urban Design studio 'Systems in the City' of this minor a sustainable urban design is the main subject. In this studio the minor students will develop design proposals, in interdisciplinary teams. Focus in the studio is on concrete sustainable design exercises. The course Basic Techniques for Sustainable Urban Design will make the minor students equipped and prepared to perform well in the design studio. They will apply theory and use their knowledge background in the group design process.

In this course you will learn:

- to recognize inner-city problems caused by climate change and social aspects;

- to recognize sustainable urban plans and sustainable measures in urban plans;

- use knowledge of climate change effects and the spatial implications of adapting to climate change in sustainable urban plans;

- to develop, integrate and evaluate spatial interventions for a future proof urban plan;

- to collaborate with students from other disciplines in order to improve own (basic) urban design skills, research skills and writing skills, and help improve these skills of others;

- to argue comprehensively for choices made in the design process, considering spatial, social and procedural circumstances;

- to give relevant feedback on content and presentation to peers, and accept and react on received feedback.

learning_objectives:
General; the student

- is able to make a design for a wide range of assignments, taking into account the relevant requirements and preconditions;

- is able to make a design based on self-formulated principles, using a wide range of research techniques and is able to critically reflect on the quality of the design;

- understands a wide range of basic technical requirements, sees them as an intrinsic part of designing and applies them as such.

Specific; the student

- is able to dissect the urban landscape into systems and networks, and integrate them in a sustainable design, on different scales; including for example local public spaces, infrastructures and buildings;

- is able to work through scales to understand sustainable systems and link to their design;

- shows an understanding of the relationship between the main urban networks, systems and the physical environment;

- can make urban design analyses based on design questions;

- can design in variants and relate these to the results of the analyses;

- is able to reflect on the social-environmental consequences of the design and take the results into account in the design;

- can document the design process in urban design impressions, plan maps, profiles and models.

description:
This course gives an introduction to the main theme of the minor, presenting the historical, theoretical and methodological frame of integrated infrastructure design and its current manifestation and future design challenges from various perspectives. Transport and water infrastructures like roads, bridges, railways, stations, waterways and dikes form the focus of the course, both as physical structures as well its system and spatial integration. The course is presented via lectures, exercises, fieldtrips and selected literature.

Content-wise, the course includes:

Key historical examples of integrated Infrastructures;

historical overview of transport and water infrastructures;

an overview of contemporary infrastructure and its integrative problems and potentials;

definitions, concepts and methods for integrated infrastructure design;

contemporary paradigms and positions in integrated infrastructure design;

hypothetical lenses for integrated infrastructure design;

learning_objectives:
At the end of the course a student:

1. has a basic knowledge of the key historical examples of integrally designed Infrastructures and can reflect on these;

2. has a rudimentary understanding of the historical development of transport and water infrastructures and can reflect on them;

3. has a working understanding of the various types of infrastructures and its integrative problems and potentials;

4. can describe and distinguish between general definitions and concepts on infrastructure design and integrated infrastructure design;

5. can identify and describe contemporary paradigms and positions in integrated infrastructure design;

6. can reflect on various hypothetical lenses in relation to integrated infrastructure design challenges;

7. is capable to review critically an infrastructure and its integrality by clear reasoning and sound arguments on basis of a clear formulated research question, a well-chosen and applied appropriate theory and on basis of relevant information and data.

At the end of the course a student:

1. has a basic knowledge of key modes of representation of infrastructure in art, design, and engineering and can reflect on these in discussions, drawings and writings;

2. can 'reverse-engineer' by means of drawing particular infrastructural artefacts;

3. is capable to interpret and reflect on non-standard cases from practice from a theoretic and design point of view;

4. can reflect on the historical and conceptual relationship between architecture and infrastructure.

The student:

1. Is capable to set up and execute a design process of an infrastructural assignment on different scale and system levels;

2. is able to derive and apply design criteria for the bridge or civil structure from the spatial context;

3. is able to derive and apply design criteria for the bridge or civil structure from technical specifications;

4. acquires knowledge of the different disciplines such as landscape design, urban design, architectural design and structural design and contributes from the own discipline to the design of a bridge or civil structure.

At the conclusion of the course a student:

- Has the ability to recognize and represent different urban systems;

- Has the ability to understand the way different urban systems are related to each other and influence each other;

- Has the ability to analyze and understand urban infrastructures as complex combinations of urban systems;

- can indicate improvements to urban infrastructures as combinations of urban systems;

- understands the historical and conceptual relationship between landscape and infrastructure;

- has a working understanding of the engineering and design considerations of 'water-scapes' in a landscape context;

- has a working understanding of the engineering and design considerations of 'road-scapes' in a landscape context;

- can develop a schematic design for an integrated flood defense element incorporating the above skills;

De student kan:

ruimtelijke composities en vormen op verschillende abstractieniveaus ontwikkelen en daarop kritisch reflecteren door middel van experimenteren en visueel denken;

kritisch en betekenisvol werken, individueel en in samenwerkingsverband, in relatie tot het eigen ontwerp en ontwerpproces (inclusief het gebruik van referenties en bronmateriaal);

(digitale) communicatie- en presentatietechnieken toepassen;

geleerde vaardigheden en bereikte ontwerpresultaten samenvatten en daarop reflecteren door middel van een portfolio.

Het doel is leren ontwerpen. Dit betekent dat de student in staat is tot:

het ontwikkelen van een betekenisvolle en aan het ontwerp richtinggevende positie ten aanzien van de technische, architectonische en duurzame toepassing van materialen en technieken;

het in samenhang toepassen en combineren van kennis in de voor deze opgave relevante aspecten - materialen, (bouw- en draag)constructie, klimaat, gebruik, ruimte, situatie en duurzaamheid - en schaalniveaus - materiële structuur en publiek gebouw;

onderzoeken in de vorm van analyseren - met name het herkennen en begrijpen van de bouwkundige principes in relatie tot de verschillende technische systemen -  en in de vorm van experimenteren - met name het ontwikkelen van ontwerpvarianten met de focus op technische, functionele en duurzame aspecten - en hierop reflecteren;

communiceren: het uitleggen, verbeelden en verantwoorden van een ontwerp en onderzoeksresultaten via handmatige schetsen, diagrammen en ruimtelijke modellen en maquettes en bouwkundige (digitale) lijntekeningen.

Na het behalen van de module kan de student:

kennis van relevante maatschappelijke opgaven en belangen van stakeholders toepassen op het programmeren van een bouwopgave;

kennis van bouweconomie toepassen op het bepalen en optimaliseren van de financiële haalbaarheid van een ontwerp;

kennis van bouwrecht toepassen op het bepalen en optimaliseren van de juridische inpasbaarheid van een ontwerp;

kennis van bouworganisatie en aanbesteden toepassen op het ontwerpen van een aanpak (organisatievoorstel, planning, contractvorm, aanbesteding);

ethische vraagstukken die kunnen spelen in de bouwpraktijk, en consequenties, bespreken.

Het doel is leren ontwerpen. Dit betekent dat de student in staat is tot:

het ontwikkelen van een betekenisvolle en aan het ontwerp richtinggevende positie ten aanzien van een gebiedsvisie en strategie, waarin een duurzame stedelijke herontwikkeling circulair, klimaatadaptief, natuur inclusief centraal staat;

het in samenhang toepassen en combineren van kennis in de voor deze opgave relevante aspecten en schaalniveaus: gebruik (programma), functie, ruimte, (milieu)techniek, recht, besliskunde, economie en (maatschappelijke) context;

onderzoeken in de vorm van analyseren, met name het formuleren en onderzoeken van relevante vragen en aspecten met betrekking tot een gebiedsvisie en strategie voor stedelijke herontwikkeling en in de vorm van experimenteren, met name het disciplinair (eigen rol) en interdisciplinair (ontwerpteam) verkennen en testen van verschillende ideeën en varianten en hierop reflecteren;

communiceren: het uitleggen, verbeelden en verantwoorden van een ontwerp en onderzoeksresultaten in overleg, presentaties en rapporten en het reflecteren op het rol- en groepswerk.

The student must be able to:

1. Employ a design task with a multidisciplinary team to solve a real technical problem in a medical environment:

translate the clinical problem as presented in the assignment into a practical, technical solution, i.e. do a problem analysis, specify design requirements, come up with a conceptual design, create a cardboard model;

obtain feedback on the clinical feasibility of the concept from the medical assignor to further detail the design;

bring "green" elements in their design related to reuse, repair, refurbish and recycle to minimise the impact on the environment during production, use and disposal (live cycle).

2. Realize the fabrication of a prototype medical device in collaboration with instrument makers and production companies:

create detailed drawings, CAD model;

formulate a production plan.

3. Evaluate the performance of the new prototype:

test the technical functionality of the device and/or the clinical applicability of the device in a medical setting;

reflect on previously made design choices based on the performance of the prototype.

4. Present the design to a multidisciplinary audience of technicians, clinicians and (medical) companies.

prepare and communicate a scientific poster;

prepare and communicate a scientific paper.

Learning objectives related to integrated design knowledge development and application:

To acquire knowledge of models, methods, and techniques for analyzing stakeholders;

To determine role and effect of stakeholders on the design process;

To develop requirements and criteria for the design of a system, component or method considering several stakeholders;

To determine the effect of proposed design on internal and external stakeholders

Learning objectives related to the trainees individual project:

To identify project requirements, their types and their importance in the trainees individual project;

To draft an appropriate stakeholder/social relevance research question and research design which contributes to the trainees individual project;

To involve relevant stakeholders through interviews and creative sessions;

To analyze and integrate adjusted and or new requirements for further improvements of the design

Learning objectives related to academic skills:

Visualizations

Prototyping

Effective communication

Involving stakeholders

Design Thinking

In Modules 1-3 the trainee learns to:

Interpret and evaluate financial statements and financial performance of companies and or businesses.

Understand and analyse the industry dynamics of technological innovation and companies technology strategy.

Gain understanding of the fundamentals of cost-benefit analysis (CBA) and know the strength and limitations in project or innovation evaluation.

Apply relevant techniques and methods for decision making on investment in development and renewal projects.

In Module 4 the trainee learns to:

Consider several alternative solutions for a specific technical challenge;

Carry out an integral assessment of the individual design or research project including technical, financial, benefit, environmental, safety aspects and other relevant facets like user friendliness, health quality and sustainability;

Define development projects regarding technical feasibility, economic feasibility, regulation and reliability. Such a project should be managed within the (financial) budgets for people, resources and systems. Decisions and design choices should be based on solid reasoning;

Present the value of a project or innovation for the organisation and outline the required financial funding and resources for the implementation.

After completion of the project the trainee should be able to:

Integrate and apply theoretical knowledge in complex and initially open-ended civil projects;

Evaluate options and systematically make choices based on qualitative and quantitative arguments, working with conflicting requirements;

Plan and organize a project with milestones and specifications of activities and tasks;

Professionally work in a team and deal with sponsors, mandators and other stakeholders;

On completion of this crossover module, the student will be able to:

    Understand, apply and critically reflect on the following construction management topics:

a.       Project management theory, methods and tools;

b.       Stakeholder management, complexity and risk management;

c.       Teamwork, systems engineering and information management.

    Develop a design for a construction project with civil, environmental and geotechnical aspects to be dealt with including technical elaboration;

    Develop an execution plan for a construction project with civil, environmental and geotechnical aspects including planning, logistics and procurement strategy;

Collaborate in a multi-disciplinary project team

The course covers three main areas:

* Transistors as the basic devices in digital computers;

* Signals and Systems (Discrete and Continuous);

* Basics of Control System Theory.

For each area, we have defined learning objectives. In the following, we list them per area.

In the area of "Transistors for Digital Computers", after following this course, the student is able to:

* explain MOSFET characteristics;

* distinguish between combinational and sequential circuit implementations;

* analyse simple logic functions in terms of area, delay and power/energy usage;

* describe simple hardware functions both at the register-transfer level (RTL) and using a hardware description language such as VERILOG;

* discuss the relationships between the transistor properties and logic gate characteristics.

In the area of " Discrete Signals and Systems", after following this course, the student is able to:

* describe and mathematically model discrete and continuous systems;

* describe system properties and classifications;

* linearize non-linear ODEs systems; and

* describe and mathematically model simple physical systems: ODEs, TF, Bode plots.

In the area of "Control Systems", after following this course, the student is able to:

* describe stability notions;

* design and analyse feedback controllers for linear systems, e.g.,: PID; and

* connect to Python commands and low level software implementations.

- The student understands the basics of reconfigurable technology;

- The student is able to design efficiently for reconfigurable technology (in terms of performance and resource utilization);

- The student can identify computationally intensive parts of applications that are suitable for acceleration using reconfigurable computing;

- The student masters state-of-the-art EDA tools, in particular HLS tools, for reconfigurable computing;

- The student will be able to approach the design problems using a top-down methodology (starting from an application, to an architecture, down to a (re)configuration bitstream);

The student after completing the course will be able to:

- derive recurrent relations from mathematical expressions;

- design a data dependency graph based on the recurrent relations;

- transform the data dependency graph into a signal flow graph using projection and scheduling;

- describe mathematical expressions that specify data dependencies and operations in a graph by means of a functional programming (FP) language;

- synthesize hardware circuits using the FP descriptions;

- describe the results of the project assignment in a report.

1. The student is able to design a research or engineering project:

- The student is able to orientate on an interesting scientific or engineering question by means of a scientific literature study;

- The student is able to use, explain and justify adequate research and design methodologies;

- The student is able to apply theory to the performed project;

- The student is able to use techniques for interpretation and verification and bases his/her conclusions on results;

- The student is able to do reliable work with scientific significance.

2. The student is able to execute a research or engineering project:

- The student has a critical attitude towards his/her own results, literature and specialists;

- The student makes an original contribution to the project;

- The student takes initiative (together with the supervisor) to give his/her own input within the research project;

- The student interacts sufficiently with peers and superiors;

- The student is able to make and execute a project plan.

3. The student is able to write a research report about the project:

- The student is able to write a research report that shows sufficient coherence of content;

- The students is able to structure the research report and sufficiently present the content (text and figures);

- The student expresses argumentation using correct spelling and grammar;

4. The student is able to present and defend the research or engineering project:

- The student is able to present the content using sufficient detail to support conclusions;

- The student is able to logical structure the presentation and use visual aids;

- The student is able to adequately formulate and express himself/herself as well as sufficiently address the audience;

- The student is able to argument and answer the questions asked by the committee.

After completing this course, the student can:

Knowledge:
1) explain with your own words basic concepts of soft materials;
2) explain how different experimental techniques can be applied to determine the structure, dynamical response and properties of a soft material;

Understanding:
3) identify various kinds of intermolecular interactions which determine the structure and dynamics of soft materials at various length scales;
4) explain how structure and dynamics of soft materials (e.g. polymers, self-assembled systems) can be used for a given product or application;

Analysis (critical thinking):
5) interpret quantitatively (characterization) experimental data on soft materials;
6) calculate basic quantities related to structure and dynamics of soft materials;

Design:
7) identify how specific properties of a given soft material determine or influence the process method chosen for a given material application
8) describe various ways of tuning material properties for desired performance in different applications.

The student:

can read and understand theory and scientific literature of a specific topic;

is able to work independently on an academic research level; planning is a keyword;

is able to work in an interdisciplinary and multicultural team of experts and supporting technologists/analysts;

has to be able to present his/her results in English by means of a presentation to a professional audience and by means of a written scientific report.

The student is able to:

Review and assess scientific literature critically;

Write a overview of the relevant literature for a specific research topic;

Provide an critical discussion of open problems on the research topic with possible solutions or strategies towards solutions for such problems;

Present and discuss the content of the literature study with specialists in this particular field of research.

On completion of this module, you should be able to:

A-LO1: Acquire conceptual (hydro)geological knowledge on project site;

A-LO2: Integrate (hydro-)geological information and knowledge into ground models and estimate resulting uncertainty for a variety of geotechnical applications

A-LO3: Formulate, explain and solve the (equations of the) finite element method in the context of numerical modelling of geotechnical applications;

A-LO4: Design and execute numerical simulations for geotechnical applications;

A-LO5: Critically evaluate the results and the limitations of model solutions for geological and geotechnical problems, considering data and model uncertainty;

A-LO6: Communicate results of geo-engineering investigation and numerical simulations to stakeholders in terms of geo-risks and opportunities, in the context of a responsible use of the subsurface for multi-purposes.

description:
HE Module B2 consists of three units:

B2-1. Physics of River Systems (4EC), which focusses on the physics of river systems and their response to anthropogenic or natural change;

B2-2. River Functions and Management (6EC), which provides students with a broad knowledge on the wide range of purposes or functions of a river system (flood safety, navigation, freshwater availability, nature, etc) and stakeholders, as well as river interventions;

B2-3. Schematization and Modelling of River Problems (5EC), which teaches students how to schematize and simplify a river-related problem, formulate research questions and alternative solutions, assess the problem using appropriate tools (such as modelling and monitoring), propose a solution and mitigate unwanted impact.

learning_objectives:
On completion of this module, the student will be able to

understand and apply scientific knowledge that contributes to solving river system-related problems in an ethically sound manner;

analyse and assess the behaviour of river systems and catchment, and assess and predict the system response to interventions, while taking into account neighbouring/adjacent systems;

(a) analyze the problem, (b) formulate functional requirements and constraints, (c) formulate alternative solutions (including autonomous development), (d) design human interventions in the river or catchment environment for purposes such as flood protection, nature development or restoration, navigation, freshwater supply, ecology, port operability, water quality, and construction in water;

account for societal, environmental, and economic aspects, and uncertainties in research and design, at various scales;

account for the complexities of multidisciplinary environments and the range of stakeholders: (inter)national river management authorities, nature organisations, NGOs, dredging companies, navigation stakeholders, other governments.

description:
Cold regions engineering relates to all engineering activities related to coping with low temperatures, specifically freezing temperatures, in the cold regions of our planet.
This course provides an adequate understanding of the unique characteristics that work in these cold regions brings. Challenges include engineering problems related to soil mechanics, hydraulics, heat flow, materials and construction, among others, in cold environments. Therefore, this module addresses a broad range of cold regions engineering design and construction activities.

Topics covered in this module are:

Physics and mechanics of sea, lake and river ice;

Ice growth, ice thermodynamics and icing;

Radiation, albedo, glacial energy budget and ice melt

Ice morphology, Ice features and ice regimes;

Cold regions structures and development;

Sea ice dynamics en remote sensing;

Ice actions on port, offshore and coastal structures;

Dynamic ice-structure interaction;

Additional topics that are shortly addressed optionally depending on time available:

Material behavior at low temperatures;

Construction on ice and permafrost: foundations, roads and airfields;

Ships in ice;

learning_objectives:
On completion of this module, the student will be able to:

Identify water related risks for flood prone regions with and without flood defence systems in different geographical features and threats i.e. riverine, coastal, mountainous and urban areas [ILO 3,5];

Perform a functional analysis of a flood protection system given its protected region [ILO 3,5].

Quantify key elements of flood risk analysis i.e. flooding probabilities and consequences [ILO 3];

Perform additional essential elements of flood risk analysis for flood prone regions: setting-up hydraulic boundary conditions, estimation of failure probability of defence systems, analysis of breach growth, (simple) overland flow modelling, damage assessment and estimation [ILO 3,10].

Formulate flood risk reduction strategies and mitigation measures based on current and future needs [ILO 1,8,9]

On completion of this module, you will be able to:

recognise and discuss architectural styles and construction methods of the 19th, 20th and 21st century;

distinguish façade typologies and use those in design, while correctly applying basic laws of building physics in the design evaluation to meet relevant criteria for climate, energy-efficiency and durability, taking into account material properties, climate data and user behaviour;

discuss the relationship and integration between design of load bearing structure, façade structure and other relevant parts/aspects of the building, such as services, vertical transport, plumbing, climate, ducts;

analyse whether reuse of existing structures or structural components in a project is economically and technically feasible and, where it is, reuse such existing building structures in the design of the newly developed building, correctly assessing the reliability of the existing structure or components based on measurements or probabilistic reasoning;

correctly identify functional, structural and construction requirements and loads for low-rise and multi-storey buildings with different targeted functions;

explain and apply, in the context of a design, the most important typologies and stability systems of low-rise and multi-storey buildings, using different construction materials, and being able to model and simulate the behaviour of such systems with existing FEM software;

develop, analyse and evaluate various reliable solutions for building foundations and deep basements in close cooperation with a geoengineer, considering the close interaction between structure and foundation, recognising and limiting risks of uncertain underground soil conditions, surveying and foundation methods;

explain how a building design can be constructed in an economic, safe and practical manner, acknowledging what the consequences and risks of construction methods are for the design and vice versa, and acting as a structural engineer with a professional, responsible and ethical attitude;

Design spatial structures (such as domes, (grid)shells, inflatables and membrane structures, space frames, cable systems and tensegrity structures), analyse and explain their 3D structural action;

Use advanced design tools, numerical analysis tools, and physical models to analyse and visualise 3D structural behaviour.

By the end of this module, students will be able to:

design safe, sustainable, resilient, and adaptable (multi-modal) transport systems;

apply transport design practices in a complex societal environment;

assess the impact of transport systems on society and vice versa; and

develop creative and innovative answers in response to transport engineering challenges.

This course focuses on the relationship between materials behaviour and structure of the material on different levels of observation, viz. nano-, micro- and mesolevel. Knowledge of phenomena acting on different levels of observation, as well as methods, both experimental and conceptual, for studying these phenomena, are dealt with. Conceptual and numerical modelling of materials behaviour is a core activity in this course.

Specific aims of the course are:

1. Acquiring insight in nano-, micro- and mesostructure of building materials;

2. Judgement and use of suitable techniques for fundamental studies of building materials, e.g. cement-based materials;

3. Numerical modelling of materials behaviour and of transport- and degradation processes in porous materials.

description:
LO1 (week 1):

Formulation on the governing equation of 3D consolidation: balance laws and constitutive laws.

Definition of initial conditions and boundary conditions.

LO2 (week 2,3):

Analysis of geotechnical problems related to 1D consolidation (loading, excavation, pumping);

Analysis of practical solutions to shorten the consolidation time (pre-loading, vertical drains, vacuum);

Interpretation of the results and limitations of the 1D approximation.

LO3 (week 4,5):

Extension of consolidation formulation to time dependent and cyclic loads;

Implementation of numerical tool to solve 1D consolidation problems with time dependent and cyclic loads (e.g. Python, Matlab);

Sensitivity analysis on the solution.

LO4 (week 6, 7):

Extension to 2D/3D problems;

Application of analytical solutions for 2D/3D coupled problems (e.g. shallow and deep foundations, tunnels)

