Education Method

Lectures, tutorial sessions, question hours, exercises, assignments, oral presentation, peer and self-evaluation, coursework, field excursions

The specific Learning Objectives for the unit MFTBP are:

LO 01: Explain the physical principles of free surface flow, turbulence, mixing and transport in open channels and the associated hydraulic loads;

LO 02: Assess and select appropriate numerical techniques for solving the equations for transport and free surface flows.

LO 03: Derive analytical/numerical models to assess typical hydraulic engineering problems regarding based on physical principles.

LO 04: Devise suitable computing methods for the physically consistent solution to flow and transport equations;

LO 05: Apply mathematical and numerical methods in combination with data to predict and assess flow and transport phenomena in hydraulic engineering;

LO 06: Design solutions for typical hydraulic engineering problems regarding scour, river contamination, and bed stability

After the completion of this unit you will be able to:

LO 01: explain the role of the river system in the hydrological cycle, the controls of a river system, time scales of change of the controls versus time scale of system response (catchment to reach scale), and phases (initial, transient, quasi-equilibrium, equilibrium) of river response

LO 02: derive and apply simplified forms of the one-dimensional shallow-water equations (i.e., normal flow, flood waves, and backwater curves);

LO 03: predict and assess (initial, equilibrium, and transient) river response to natural and anthropogenic change of controls

LO 04: identify and assess the range of river functions (e.g. flood safety, navigation, nature restoration, freshwater supply, ecology, port operability, water quality, and construction in water), system-related problems (from catchment to reach scale) in national and international river management, and possible solutions or interventions

LO 05: apply (inter)national safety standards in flood risk management;

The specific Learning Objectives for the unit Introduction to Ports and Waterways Systems

LO 01 - general:

Explain the functions of ports and waterways in the total transport chain to a level that is necessary for functional design and capacity analysis;

Discuss the importance of performance indicators such as capacity, efficiency, safety and sustainability;

Describe the environmental issues relevant to ports and waterways and explain the importance of integrating environmental considerations in (stakeholder inclusive) port planning and design.

LO 02 - ports/terminals:

Explain the principles of port master planning and develop basic port layouts based on principles of robustness and adaptability in an uncertain future;

Develop functional designs of port terminals (number of elements and their order-of-magnitude dimensions) using state-of-the-art design methods and rules (Module A focuses on container terminals, Module B04-01 will address other terminal types);

Explain the basic elements of investment analysis and can perform such an analysis for a simple case (analysing trade-offs).

LO 03 - waterways and port water areas:

Discuss the importance of inland waterway transport corridor policies/plans and perform a basic capacity analysis taking into account forecasted traffic volumes and waterway infrastructure in place or planned;

Explain the basics of ship motions, ship-ship interaction and ship-fairway interaction;

Develop functional designs of inland waterways and port waterway areas using state-of-the-art design methods and rules (canals, locks, bridges, approach channels, turning basins, berths).

LO 04 - system behaviour:

Analyse relevant processes influencing capacity;

Compare the performance of port and waterway systems using rules of thumb and queueing theory (waiting time as a fraction of service time) and the Kooman method for lock capacity assessment.

The course provides an overview of (global) supply chains, various types of ports, various aspects of waterways, and simulation methods to analyse and optimize the capacity of the system. Upon completion of the course, the student is able to:

1. analyze and evaluate logistic and operational processes in ports and waterways at a level that is relevant for functional design and capacity analysis of (global) supply chains;

2. develop the functional design and basic layout given the preset requirements for various types of port terminals (general cargo, ro/ro; liquid bulk, dry bulk, marinas, fishery ports, marinas, and IWT ports), and motivate the design choices made (fulfillment of design criteria including current and future demand, and local and overall performance);

3. develop the functional design given the preset preset requirements for various types of waterway systems (a canal with locks, bridges, etc.), and motivate the design choices made (fulfillment of criteria including current and future demand, and local and overall performance);

4. analyse/optimize water-based logistical processes in ports and waterways systems by applying deterministic and stochastic nautical traffic simulation models (capacity analysis, integration of engineering knowledge, awareness of non-intuitive feedback);

5. discuss the importance of nautical safety and summarize the functions of maritime and inland Vessel Traffic Management Systems, and

6. summarize the most significant future developments for ports and waterways design and optimization, and understand the (potential) implications of these developments for port and waterway developers.

1. Knowledge of the behaviour of concrete structures in the early stage of hardening, including measures to influence this behaviour (through technological and structural measures);

2. Design of concrete structures subjected to imposed deformations (temperature, shrinkage);

3. Design and execution of storage systems in reinforced and prestressed concrete;

4. Liquid tight design of concrete structures;

5. Safety considerations in case of storage of hazardous product, i.e. liquefied natural gas, concrete containment structure (nuclear power plants), hazardous waste etc.

6. Assetmanagement concepts for concrete structures

After following this course the student:

Has knowledge of the history of architecture and of the historical developments in steel, wood and concrete structures;

Knows the most important developments in urban planning, architecture, typology and styles of the 19th and 20th century;

Understands the relation between architectural development of this period and the development of technology;

Has basic knowledge of the history of building codes and understands how the evolution of codes influences the expected safety of existing structures;

Can assess the structural safety of existing buildings;

Understands systems, theories and possibilities of restoration, renovation, re-use and re-design of buildings and constructions;

understands how to apply historical knowledge and knowledge of the structure to a design;

Has knowledge of how existing buildings or complexes of buildings can be systematically inventoried and analysed on the basis of architectural, structural, environmental, cultural and historical value propositions;

Is able to redesign assignments from an integral vision and make argued and reasoned choices on principles in relation to the existing situation.

CIEQ5251A contains a subset of the learning objectives of the larger module CIEM5250 Building Engineering (15 ECTS), taken from Learning Unit 1 "Architecture, Building Physics and Facades" (4 EC) and Learning Unit 2 "Building Structures, Construction Methods and Foundations" (6 EC).

CIEQ5251B contains a subset of the learning objectives of CIEM5250 Building Engineering (15 ECTS), all taken from Learning Unit 1 "Architecture, Building Physics and Façades".

On completion of CIEQ5251B, you will be able to:

1. recognise and discuss architectural styles and construction methods of the 19th, 20th and 21st century;

2. distinguish façade typologies and use those in design, while correctly applying the laws of building physics in the design evaluation to meet relevant criteria for climate, energy-efficiency and durability, taking into account stochastic material properties, climate data and user behaviour;

3. discuss the relationship and integration between design of load bearing structure, façade structure and other relevant parts/aspects of the building, such as services, vertical transport, plumbing, climate, ducts.

On completion of this module, you will be able to (from CIEM5250):

3. discuss the relationship and integration between design of load bearing structure, façade structure and other relevant parts/aspects of the building, such as services, vertical transport, plumbing, climate, ducts;

4. analyse whether reuse of existing structures or structural components in a project is economically and technically feasible and, where it is, reuse such existing building structures in the design of the newly developed building, correctly assessing the reliability of the existing structure or components based on measurements or probabilistic reasoning;

5. correctly identify functional, structural and construction requirements and loads for low-rise and multi-storey buildings with different targeted functions;

6. explain and apply, in the context of a design, the most important typologies and stability systems of low-rise and multi-storey buildings, using different construction materials, and being able to model and simulate the behaviour of such systems both with self-written code and existing FEM software;

7. develop, analyse and evaluate various reliable solutions for building foundations and deep basements in close cooperation with a geoengineer, considering the close interaction between structure and foundation, recognising and limiting risks of uncertain underground soil conditions, surveying and foundation methods;

8. explain how a building design can be constructed in an economic, safe and practical manner, acknowledging what the consequences and risks of construction methods are for the design and vice versa, and acting as a structural engineer with a professional, responsible and ethical attitude;

The main goal if for the student to understand and apply optimisation

in the context of transport problems

At the end of this course:

1) The student will be able to formulate an optimisation problem in mathematical programming;

2) The student will be able to solve an optimisation problem in mathematical programming;

3) The student will be able to reproduce typical transport optimisation problems, be able to explain their formulation, and solve some of its instances;

4) The student will be able to propose optimisation formulations to solve real-world problems.

description:
The concept of accessibility and its relevance for user behaviour and transport network and spatial design;

Spatial and social external effects of transport infrastructures and use, and its relevance for transport network and spatial design;

Characteristics, data and data analysis of population, economic activity and land use;

Interactions in regional, land use and transport network developments and their dynamics in time;

Urban and regional transport demand models;

Transport networks and regional development optimisation and design problems;

Introduction and application of Transport Economics, System Dynamics, and Agent-Based Modelling.

Economic activities, trade, logistics and freight flows;

Logistics networks with consolidation and transhipment points;

Modelling of freight transport demand and supply;

Unimodal and intermodal freight transport networks;

Freight transport and logistics optimisation from the user perspective;

Terminal and port operations;

Urban freight transport, urban distribution;

Sustainability effects of freight transport, sustainability policy consequences for freight transport

Transport Economics (applied)

Modelling and assessment of the resilience, reliability, robustness and sustainability of transport networks and designing preventive and responsive measures for improving the network performance.

Defining indicators and modelling sustainability of transport;

Defining indicators and modelling resilience of transport;

Assessment of sustainability and resilience of transport networks via Societal Cost-Benefit and Multi Criteria Analysis approaches;

(Re) Design of transport networks to improve resilience, including climate adaptation, and to improve sustainability performance, including climate mitigation.

This course will not be offered in full for the 2025/26 academic year. There will be no educational activities, only exam opportunities. For more information on supporting materials and possible contact hours, please check the Brightspace page of this course from 2024/25 (Go to Brightspace: Module CIEM6220-24 Road Traffic Systems (2024/25 Q4): U2: Traffic Safety). From 2026/27 onwards, the course will be offered in full in Q1.

Road safety paradigms en road safety research

Overview research methods in road safety;

History of road safety research;

Models and theories in road safety: road user behaviour, road design, safe systems approaches;

Traffic safety (accident) modelling;

Traffic safety and road design for vulnerable road users (cyclists, pedestrians);

Surrogate safety measures and extreme value theory;

Road safety in low and middle income countries;

New technology, automated driving and traffic safety;

Writing a research proposal.

learning_objectives:
On completion of this course, the student will be able to:

B2U2-LO1. Explain the different behavioural theories and models in the field of traffic safety;

B2U2-LO2. Explain the different research and evaluation methods applied in the field of road user behaviour and traffic safety;

B2U2-LO3. Analyse the contributing factors, characteristics and mechanisms of a selected traffic safety problem;

B2U2-LO4. Evaluate the strengths and weaknesses of different data collection and research methodologies and their ethical implications;

B2U2-LO5. Apply the basic concepts, behavioural theories and models, and the research methods to the selected traffic safety problem;

B2U2-LO6. Develop a research proposal by conducting a literature review on the selected traffic safety problem, defining research gaps, defining research objectives, and research method;

B2U2-LO7. Write a clear and reproducible scientific research proposal;

B2U2-LO8. Evaluate strengths and weaknesses of a peer group research proposal;

B2U2-LO9. Present concisely (pitch-like) to your peers the traffic safety problem and the developed research proposal.

description:
This course will not be offered in full for the 2025/26 academic year. There will be no educational activities, only the opportunity to submit the project. For more information on supporting materials, please check the Brightspace page of "CIEM6220-24 Road traffic systems" from 2024/25, and look at the material of Unit 3.

From 2026/27 onwards, the course will be offered in full in Q1.

In the Brightspace page you can find the following information:

Introduction, classification and functional description of intelligent vehicles;

New mobility services and system level impacts;

Motion planing, decision and control methods for intelligent vehicle systems;

Technologies for intelligent vehicles, sensors, communication, and actuators;

Behavioural adaptation to intelligent vehicles;

Impact assessment of intelligent vehicles on traffic safety;

Impact assessment of intelligent vehicles on traffic efficiency;

Experimental design to research impacts of intelligent vehicles on traffic flow using traffic simulation

learning_objectives:
On completion of this course, the student will be able to:

B2U4-LO1. Describe and apply macroscopic traffic flow models, such as the Cell Transmission Model, node models, to determine the evolution of traffic over time;

B2U4-LO2. Discuss the shape of the macroscopic fundamental diagram and explain perimeter control;

B2U4-LO3. Apply and explain Kalman filtering for traffic state estimation problems;

B2U4-LO4. Explain the different purposes and designs of (1) coordinated ramp metering, (2) route guidance, (3) variable speed limit systems.

Explain and apply the discussed designs for such systems to a traffic situation.

B2U4-LO5. Design and analyse a vehicle-actuated intersection controller.

B2U4-LO6. Describe and discuss the main features of network-oriented urban signal control approaches.

B2U4-LO7. Explain the steps of the design methodology for traffic control systems. Apply the steps to design problems.

Upon successful completion of this subject, the student should be able to:

1. To identify the basic elements such as project characteristics, control systems, methodology and supporting systems in an integrated design en construction process for civil engineering structures;

2. To identify characteristics dictating the way a construction project is being managed in practice and emphasis on the construction methods to be adopted when worked out;

3. To apply the basic concepts of Systems Engineering in a civil engineering project;

4. To optimise the process of design and construction in terms of costs, time and execution method in selecting a construction process, a construction schedule and investment in temporary works (eg. cranes, special equipment, formwork, temporary supporting structures);

5. To demonstrate actions which can be taken to control the design process and to assure the quality of the engineering process and the construction process;

6. To generate different design en construction concepts and to select one of them in view of costs, execution time and durability;

7. To implement all these aspects in a real-life case study.

Finishing the course, that students are able to:

- Understand and analyse an innovation case, with innovation theory to identify the successes and failures on its adoption;

- Understand specific market structures of the civil engineering domain, the relations between public and private roles in this domain, and the multifaceted reality thereof;

- Understand the market processes, specifically the public tendering processes for innovation-oriented projects;

- Analyse the technical aspects of a technology / innovation to identify the robustness and the alternative implementations thereof;

- Analyse the operational context of the projected implementations of a technology;

- Create the role of your business in the pursuit of implementing the technology / innovation;

- Create an adaptive and embedding business plan (flexible and future oriented) for the case.

- From the perspective of a client or an established market party: be able to identify, stimulate, guide and embrace innovative innovation concepts.

description:
Systems Engineering Design (SED) is a powerful design approach to get from a semi-unstructured multi-stakeholder set of vague and messy goals and needs to a well-defined set of requirements and complementary socio-technical systems. In this course a best fit for common purpose design methodology to design for complex interconnected systems using multi-objective optimization techniques. Within this approach both the subject preference function model (human) and the physical object behavior model (engineering asset) will be integrated. In other words, to design socio-technical systems one needs a sound and integrative mathematical approach (open glass box modelling) to support participatory decision making on new engineering artefacts (open source participation and open ended system theory).

It will be demonstrated that this best fit for common purpose design methodology also can be used for Project management and/ or Operations management planning applications.

The alternative view within this course is to combine multi-objective (non-)linear systems optimization and the theory preference function modeling in a constructivist design methodology, using mathematical simulation and modelling. This Open Design Systems approach is distinctive from pure engineering optimization methods. Within this approach the main concepts of systems design for tY-s, decision and development are linked with a self chosen System of Interest (SoI):

1. Systems theory and thinking and design as problem (dis)solving;

2. Hard engineering asset systems (technical-physical) vs. soft human management

systems (social);

3. A posteriori multi-actor design decision systems;

4. A priori multi-actor design decision systems;

5. Non-linearity in mathematical models for design/decision systems;

6. Best fit for common purpose.

For more course info see : https://www.open-design.school/

This course has a constructivist character, in which the student is guided and encouraged to develop open design knowledge en solutions (openings) using the self-chosen System of Interest (SoI) as a connective and practicing vehicle enabling the Open Design Learning (ODL) response.

The SoI can a transportation-, water management-, energy infrastructure and/or a real-estate system.

The climate emergency, rapid urbanisation and resultant need to provide more affordable housing quickly, along with the urgency to transform our food, water, energy and waste infrastructure are key societal and sustainability transitions of our time. Furthermore, technological advancements in the digital space and the changing demographics are also demanding a rethink in the ways organisations function. Moreover, successive crises such as the Global Financial Crisis in the late 2000s and the more recent Covid-19 Global Pandemic have triggered calls for radical change. Put another way, if we keep doing what we have always done, then we will always get the (unsustainable) results that we have always gotten. Thus, there is a need to do things differently, and this is where leadership and strategic management can play a significant role.

In tackling the societal and sustainability transitions highlighted above, simply relying on technical knowledge is not enough. These transition challenges are wicked problems, where simple solutions and incremental change are not sufficient. To address these transitions, there is also a need to inspire people from different backgrounds to activate radical change in our actions. Leadership skills are therefore critical to influence others to take the path less travelled. In addition, projects are increasingly considered and commissioned as drivers of change. Therefore, leadership is deeply connected to the delivery of projects.

The key topics of this course include:

Context: Understanding the context of leadership and strategy in transitions;

Leadership theories: Complementing management and leadership, and shifting the mindset from planning and control (outputs) to influencing change and transformation (outcomes);

Strategic management: Shifting the framing from strategy formulation and (long-term) planning to purpose-driven implementation and strategy-as-practice;

Creativity and innovation: Shifting towards more open forms of innovation, and the role leadership and organisational strategy can play;

Communication and negotiation: Understanding the power of communication and negotiation practices to influence outcomes;

Values and impacts: Critically reflecting on the value, values and impacts of leadership and strategic management.

learning_objectives:
At the end of this course you should be able to:

LO1: explain the role of public clients and public values in construction;

LO2: analyse the context of procuring construction activity in a public context;

LO3: critically reflect on the application of delivery methods;

LO4: design a procurement strategy;

LO5: writing a scientifically based advisory report using scientific literature.

The students knowledge and skill in the following activities will be increased relative to the intellectual development level attained during his or her BSc study:

The understanding of the principles of project agreements;

The understanding and choice of forms of collaboration;

The evaluation of alternative forms of contract;

The various types of reimbursement;

The selection and choice of contract partners;

The understanding of the various components of contracts;

International contract types.

By completing the CME Research & Development Project, you are able to:

Familiarize yourself with fundamental research and developments related to the topic;

Delineate a research, development or design problem and formulate a methodology to address the research or development statement in a well-structured way;

Plan, execute and manage the project effectively within the available time and resources

Demonstrate your understanding of the scientific, societal and ethical context of the CME R&D Project;

Verify, interpret, and critically reflect on obtained results;

Draw relevant conclusions and give useful recommendations;

Present the CME R&D Project in a well-structured manner;

description:
This research methodology course is intended for all CME students. The course will cover a variety of topics, such as:

problem identification;

distinction between research and development projects and processes;

defining R&D objectives;

formulating R&D questions/statements;

setting up an R&D framework;

selecting fit-for-purpose research methods associated with data collection and analysis;

Selecting fit-for-purpose development methods including supporting research methods.

Reflecting on findings of R&D project.

A broad palette of R&D methods will be introduced and discussed such as literature review, case studies, mixed methods, qualitative comparative analysis, Q-methodology, development methods for decision support systems, digital construction, etcetera.

Furthermore, the course will provide attention to skills and practices such as R&D planning, ethics, and reporting and presenting R&D project.

This course is meant for students in the bridging programme of the CME master programme who have finished their bachelors programme, but did not take a technical writing course in their bachelor's education (e.g., Civil Engineering students from TU Delft, and Civil Engineering students from Dutch universities of applied sciences (hogescholen).

Modern engineers are required to possess excellent communication skills. Not only do they need to know their business, but they also need to know how to effectively communicate their knowledge and plans to other experts, decision-makers, and implementers. They need to be able to write accessible technical reports and proposals and to hit the correct tone in professional correspondence.

Prospective engineers will benefit from this course, which focuses on both the quality of the text and the writing process itself. The following subjects will be dealt with:

a systematic approach to writing;

requirements for technical reports and their components;

structuring texts on several levels;

writing convincingly;

writing "proper and appropriate" language;

referring to scientific literature;

referencing.

Lectures and a teacher's consultation will be used for instruction, feedback, and practice. Students will be required to submit several assignments while working on the technical report, which must be completed to fulfil the course requirements. Attendance is mandatory.

learning_objectives:
After completing the CME Master Thesis, you have shown that you are able to:

Summarize relevant state-of-the-art research and developments and relate this to the thesis topic;

Demonstrate the ability to independently determine and apply relevant theory to the construction industry;

Manage the thesis process effectively considering the available time and resources;

Execute an applicable methodology aimed at reaching the thesis objectives

Effectively collaborate and communicate professionally and in an ethically and morally responsible manner

Verify, interpret, and reflect on obtained results in a critical manner;

Critically reflect on the thesis project at the level of your peers;

Present the thesis project in a well-structured written report or paper

Give a well-structured oral presentation regarding the thesis project to a wide audience that next to your peers, may include audience not directly familiar with the thesis topic;

Defend your work, results and reflections in presence of your peers by placing the thesis project into its scientific context and by explaining its contribution to the construction industry;

description:
Web en Database Technologies is a first-year course that provides an introduction to web and database technologies and programming.

Topics related to web technology and programming include:

Introduction to the web and HTTP;

Introduction to web development and app design;

Front-end development: HTML, CSS, JavaScript;

Back-end development: Node.js.

Topics related to database technologies include:

Introduction to databases;

Overview of database Languages and architectures;

The basic relational model; database design theory;

SQL: data definition, constraints, updates, queries, views, triggers;

Relational databases and object persistence;

Introduction to non-relational database models: key-value stores, document stores, graph databases.

The course offers an introduction to all aspects of landside accessibility through assessing performances of transport modes and operational systems, related to transport to and from the airport. This includes elements of infrastructure and transport service networks, for which have to be considered: demand, capacity, spatial layout, economics, environmental and social impacts, local infrastructural development, and the complex integration of airports into regional and national transport system(s).

Amongst others, the lectures will contain:

Airport and airport systems and operations, including crowd monitoring and management in airports;

Landside access modes and systems related to airports: the conventional and advanced road en rail-based components, its operations and performances;

Performances of airport access modes, considering: infrastructural, technical/technological, operational, economic, environmental, social and policies;

Control and management of landside access modes: introduction into ITS (Intelligent Transport Solutions);

Planning and design of landside access modes and systems;

Developing an airport as the multimodal transport node.

learning_objectives:
After successful completion of the course, the student is able to:

identify the characteristics and performances of transport modes providing landside access to an airport;

analyse, model, evaluate, plan, and design of airport landside access modes;

analyse and discuss the role of airports as multimodal transport modes in transport networks;

analyse and discuss urban planning and developments around airports.

The goals of the course are to understand the impacts of climate change on humans and the environment and to understand the effectiveness and limitations of adaptation options being considered.

At the end of the course, students will be able to:

(LO1) describe the impacts and adaptation of climate change on different sectors identified by the IPCC;

(LO2) Explain the effect of climate change on: freshwater resources; coastal regions (low lying areas); the occurrence of droughts and heatwaves;

(LO3) Identify potential adaptation strategies to: ensure freshwater availability, and allow life in coastal regions and under new climate conditions;

(LO4) design adaptation measures for a case study site.

At the end of the course a student:

has proven to be capable in applying the acquired knowledge and skills of the other courses of the minor into the final project;

is capable to (re)define and analyze a design issue for an infrastructural object considering different contexts and scales from the point of view of integration;

is capable to create an innovative and integrated infrastructural design for a bridge, tunnel, viaduct, dike, station or other civil structure regarding its functional, structural and architectural design and which is well integrated within the transport and/or water system and within the urban fabric and/or natural environment;

is capable to work methodically in a design project in collaboration with students from different disciplinary backgrounds.

By the end of this course, you will be able to:

prepare your own asphalt sample;

determine the density and air voids content;

translate experimental results of asphalt concrete mixtures into pavement response predictions using a computational pavement analysis software;

explain the difference in stiffness as determined by diverse measurement techniques (4-point-bending and 3-point-bending tests)

Kennis van basisbegrippen en definities binnen het transportsysteem;

kennis van de verschillende fijnmazige vervoermodaliteiten m.n. weg- en railvervoer;

kennis van het ladingselement: personen en goederen;

technische kennis van vervoermiddelen;

technische kennis van planning, ontwerp en constructie van verkeersinfrastructuur;

inzicht in de vervoermarkt en de verkeersmarkt

description:
Het vak geeft een introductie van het werkgebied van stedelijke en transportplanners.

Colleges en workshops

Onderwerpen die aan de orde komen zijn:

Stedelijke ontwikkeling

Historische stedelijke ontwikkeling in samenhang met de ontwikkeling van transportsystemen en - netwerken;

Analyse en Ontwerptechnieken voor stedelijke gebieden w.o. Nolli, Morfologisch, Lynch, en Cullen (kwalitatief);

Transportsystemen

Infrastructuur, vervoermiddelen (voertuigen), diensten en gerelateerde ruimtelijke effecten;

Relaties tussen vervoervraag, transportdiensten en het verkeerssysteem;

Analyse en modelleertechnieken, w.o. 4-stapsmodel, microsimulatie, (systeem-) dynamische analyse (kwalitatief);

Kenmerken van transport- en verkeersnetwerken: netwerkelementen, hiërarchie in netwerken, schalen, combineren en integreren van subsystemen.

Stedelijke ontwikkeling en transportsystemen

Relaties tussen gebiedskarakteristieken, vervoervraag en vervoer en verkeer;

Relatie tussen stedelijke ontwikkeling en infrastructuur: infrastructuur als leidend en als volgend element; de inertie van infrastructuur;

Verschillende perspectieven op stedelijke ontwikkeling i.r.t. vervoersystemen en -concepten, o.a.: Grids, Garden Cities, Transit Oriented Development, 15 Minutes City (incl. aandacht voor 'active modes').

Praktijkvoorbeelden

Herstructurering van voormalige havengebieden, openbaar vervoer en stedelijke ontwikkeling, ontwikkeling binnensteden.

Oefening (groepsopdracht)

Analyseren en ontwerpen van geïntegreerd stedelijk ruimtelijk plan en verkeerssysteem in een oefening.

learning_objectives:
After the course the student should be able to:

recognize challenges in solid waste management;

recognize the potential and limitations of recycling routes;

have basic knowledge of modern solid waste processing techniques;

discuss aspects of solid waste recycling with pears;

place acquired knowledge in the context of circular economy.

After the course the student should be able to:

have basic knowledge of biomass as a nutrient and as energy source;

calculate basic physical and chemical properties of biomass flows and energy conversion rates;

discuss biomass challenges with peers;

recognise the environmental and climate related implications of biomass.

Onderdeel Werkboek

M.b.t. rapporteren kan de student:

 Een effectief technisch rapport schrijven;

 Een effectieve structuur voor een technisch rapport opzetten;

 Een effectieve introductie en samenvatting schrijven;

 Overtuigende conclusies en aanbevelingen schrijven;

 Een duidelijke en aantrekkelijke schrijfstijl gebruiken;

 Functionele illustraties en tabellen gebruiken.

M.b.t. de kennisverwerving kan de student:

 de kennisbehoefte vaststellen.

 gericht zoeken door middel van een zoekplan.

 de gevonden informatie beoordelen op bruikbaarheid en betrouwbaarheid.

 informatie bronnen weergeven in een database en in groepsverband beheren.

M.b.t. tekenen kan de student:

 Het vaardig gebruiken van potlood, pennen, stiften, liniaal, tekenpapier, schetsrol

 Kijken en vertalen naar papier: het snel kunnen omzetten van een object dat je in het echt ziet (bijv. gebouw, brug, weg, sluis, viaduct, constructie of onderdelen hiervan) in een 2D of 3D handschets

 Ontwerpen: het snel kunnen maken van een 2D of 3D handschets van (een onderdeel van) een zelf bedachte technische oplossing

 Het hebben dan wel ontwikkelen van ruimtelijk inzicht

 Het kunnen communiceren met anderen op basis van schetsen en uitwerking hiervan in een technische tekening

 Begrijpen en toepassen van de Amerikaanse projectie;

 Ruimtelijk inzicht en het vertalen van 3D naar 2D;

 Opbouw van een technische tekening;

 Maken van een eenvoudige technische tekening;

 Toepassen van de tekenregels;

 Aanduiding van details in tekeningen;

 Intekenen van betonwapening;

 Nieuwe vormen van maataanduiding;

 Maataanduidingen door middel van bijschriften;

 De opbouw van de hoofddraagconstructie van een eenvoudige hal begrijpen.

Onderdeel Tekenen en Planning

De fasering van een project kunnen benoemen;

Samenwerken in een projectgroep;

Resultaten presenteren;

Aan te geven waar de eigen voorkeuren en kwaliteiten liggen m.b.t. de werkzaamheden in de verschillende fasen;

de inpassing van subsystemen (bouwwerken) in een omgeving vaststellen en weergeven;

de vorm van een ontwerp vertalen in de werking (hetgeen er wordt bewerkstelligd), de wijze van opbouw, werking en capaciteiten;

de verschillen zien tussen een systeem, subsysteem en sub-subsysteem (van grof naar fijn);

de werkelijkheid kritisch opnemen en weergeven;

Het effect van het gebruik op de omgeving weergeven en dit vertalen in ofwel een knelpunt of randvoorwaarden voor toekomstige plannen;

Herkennen eigen voorkeur qua rol in een project;

Reflecteren op individuele inbreng in groepsproces;

Project op systeem- en subsysteemniveau in kaart brengen aan de hand van plattegronden, schetsen en tekeningen;

Een ontwerp inpassen in de omgeving;

Een besluitvormingsproces in een groep doorgronden en beïnvloeden;

Het opstellen van een Work breakdown Structure (WBS);

Het maken van een projectplanning dmv planningssoftware;

Rhino

basisvaardigheden Rhino;

3D schetsen met de computer;

presentatie-afbeeldingen maken.

Onderdeel Diverse Software

Weten wat BIM is;

Weten wat er nodig is om BIM te kunnen toepassen bij een bouwproject;

Het bekend zijn met de (on)mogelijkheden van BIM / BEM

Een constructie kunnen modelleren met Tekla;

Een constructie kunnen modelleren met Revit;

Een materialenstaat kunnen uittrekken uit een BIM-model;

Op efficiënte wijze technische tekeningen en presentatietekeningen kunnen maken vanuit een BIM-model.

Onderdeel CAD/BIM/BEM

Het begrijpen van de consequenties van het gebruik van BIM / BEM voor het ontwerpproces

Het binnen een groep ontwerpers op effectieve wijze kunnen maken van afspraken over versies, mutaties, bevoegdheden, beheer, etc.

Modelinformatie kunnen uitwisselen tussen verschillende modelleerpakketten;

Clashcontrole kunnen uitvoeren;

Het kunnen uitwerken en visualiseren van eenvoudige 4D of 5D informatie op grond van het model

description:
Deel 1:

Eerste en tweede orde differentiaalvergelijkingen;

Inverse goniometrische functies;

Differentiëren;

Integratietechnieken;

Complexe getallen.

Deel 2:

Vergelijkingen van lijnen en vlakken;

Functies van meerdere variabelen;

Partiële afgeleiden;

Richtingsafgeleide;

Extreme waarden van functies;

Meervoudige integralen;

Coördinatensystemen.

learning_objectives:
Na een succesvolle afronding van dit vak is de student in staat om:

een wiskundig model op te stellen met behulp van differentiaalvergelijkingen (DVen);

een eerste orde DV te visualiseren als een richtingsveld;

eerste orde separeerbare en lineaire DVen op te lossen;

met een tweede orde lineaire DV een gedwongen trilling te modelleren, deze op te lossen en het resultaat daarvan te interpreteren;

tweede orde lineaire DVen met constante coëfficiënten op te lossen;

oplossingen van DVen te benaderen met Taylorpolynomen;

het inwendig en uitwendig product van twee vectoren te berekenen en deze te gebruiken in andere berekeningen;

vergelijkingen en parametervoorstellingen op te stellen van lijnen en vlakken in de ruimte;

het domein van een functie van meerdere variabelen te bepalen, de partiële afgeleiden te berekenen en raakvlakken te bepalen;

de gradiënt en extreme waarden te bepalen van een functie van meerdere variabelen, de meetkundige betekenis van de gradiënt om te schrijven, richtingsafgeleiden met behulp van de gradiënt te berekenen;

meervoudige integraal uit te rekenen;

te beslissen of tweevoudige en drievoudige integraal handiger kan worden berekend met behulp van coördinatensystemen als pool-, cilinder- en bolcoördinaten en deze berekening uit te voeren.

description:
Het vak CTB1002 bestaat uit 2 deelvakken, met elk een eigen deeltentamen:

In Q3: Lineaire Algebra 1 (deeltentamen T1)

In Q4: Lineaire Algebra 2 (deeltentamen T2)

CTB1002 - T1 Lineaire Algebra 1 (deeltentamen):

Stelsels lineaire vergelijkingen en echelonvormen;

Vectorvergelijkingen en opspansels;

Matrix-vectorproduct en oplossingsverzamelingen;

Lineaire onafhankelijkheid;

Lineaire afbeeldingen;

Matrixalgebra;

Inverteerbaarheid van een matrix;

Lineaire deelruimten;

Coördinaten en dimensie;

Inwendig product en orthogonaliteit;

Orthogonale projecties;

Gram-Schmidt-procedure en QR-decompositie;

Kleinstekwadratenmethode;

Lineaire modellen.

CTB1002 - T2 Lineaire Algebra 2 (deeltentamen):

Determinant, oppervlak en volume;

Determinanten en matrix operaties;

Eigenwaarden, eigenvectoren en eigenruimten;

Karakteristieke vergelijking en gelijksoortigheid;

Diagonaliseerbaarheid;

Complexe eigenwaarden;

Discrete dynamische systemen;

Stelsels van lineaire differentiaalvergelijkingen;

Symmetrische matrices;

learning_objectives:
CTB1002 - Lineaire Algebra 1

Na het succesvol afronden van dit vak kun je:

nagaan of een gegeven stelsel lineaire vergelijkingen consistent of inconsistent is en in geval van consistentie de volledige oplossingsverzameling bepalen m.b.v. de methode van het reduceren van de bijbehorende aangevulde matrix naar een echelonvorm;

een stelsel vergelijkingen interpreteren als een vectorvergelijking en vice versa;

een stelsel vergelijkingen interpreteren als een matrixvergelijking en vice versa;

de algemene oplossing van een consistent stelsel schrijven in vectorvorm;

nagaan of een gegeven verzameling vectoren lineair afhankelijk of lineair onafhankelijk is;

nagaan of een afbeelding van Rn naar Rm een lineaire afbeelding is;

de standaardmatrix bepalen van een gegeven lineaire afbeelding van Rn naar Rm;

berekeningen uitvoeren in matrixalgebra (optellen van matrices, vermenigvuldigen van een matrix met een getal, vermenigvuldigen van matrices, transponeren van een matrix);

nagaan of een vierkante matrix inverteerbaar is en de inverse uitrekenen in geval van inverteerbaarheid;

nagaan of een gegeven deelverzameling van Rn wel of niet een lineaire deelruimte is;

de dimensie en een basis bepalen van de nulruimte en de kolomruimte van een matrix;

de coördinaten uitrekenen van een vector in een lineaire deelruimte t.o.v. een basis van die deelruimte;

berekeningen uitvoeren m.b.t. het inwendig product;

nagaan of een gegeven verzameling vectoren een orthogonale/orthonormale verzameling is;

de orthogonale projectie uitrekenen van een vector op een deelruimte;

werken met matrices met orthonormale kolommen;

een basis van een lineaire deelruimte omzetten naar een orthogonale basis m.b.v. de Gram-Schmidt-procedure;

een QR-decompositie opstellen voor een matrix en deze gebruiken in verschillende contexten;

een inconsistent stelsel oplossen in de zin van de kleinstekwadratenmethode;

meervoudige regressie uitvoeren op een gegeven verzameling punten.

CTB1002 - Lineaire Algebra 2

Na het succesvol afronden van dit vak kun je:

de determinant uitrekenen van een matrix;

oppervlaktes en volumes uitrekenen m.b.v. determinanten;

de karakteristieke vergelijking van een matrix opstellen;

de eigenwaarden van een matrix berekenen;

voor elke eigenwaarde van een gegeven matrix bijbehorende eigenvectoren en eigenruimte bepalen;

nagaan of een matrix diagonaliseerbaar is en in geval van diagonaliseerbaarheid de diagonalisatie uitvoeren;

een reële (2x2)-matrix met niet-reële eigenwaarden in standaardvorm brengen (positief veelvoud van rotatie-matrix) m.b.v. een gelijkvormigheidstransformatie;

het gedrag van zowel discrete-tijd als continue-tijd dynamische systemen analyseren m.b.v. de eigendata van de bijbehorende matrix en oplossingen hiervan schetsen;

een symmetrische matrix orthogonaal diagonaliseren;

een kwadratische vorm op hoofdassen brengen;

nagaan of een kwadratische vorm positief (semi-)definiet, negatief (semi-)definiet of indefiniet is;

niveaukrommen van kwadratische vormen op R2 schetsen;

een singulierewaardenontbinding uitvoeren voor een willekeurige matrix.

Na afronding van dit vak moet de student specifiek in staat zijn om:

De begrippen, basisgrootheden en concepten binnen verkeer, vervoer en planning toe te passen in analyses en ontwerp;

Modellen voor verkeers- en vervoerssystemen toe te passen om deze systemen te analyseren dimensioneren;

Beargumenteerd kiezen van geschikte databronnen / en -inwinning voor verkeer en vervoer, en interpreteren van die data;

Effecten van het verkeer- en vervoerssysteem te benoemen en te beoordelen op hun primaire doelen, en in relatie tot maatschappelijke waarden met betrekking tot ethiek, duurzaamheid en welzijn;

Ethische dilemma’s tussen stakeholders bij transport gerelateerde onderwerpen herkennen en beschrijven;

Effectief bijdragen aan een groepswerk om oplossingen te ontwikkelen voor uitdagingen binnen ruimtelijke planning en verkeers- en vervoersoplossingen.

Na afronding van dit vak moet de student specifiek in staat zijn om:

Leerdoelen (LD):

LD1: Het herkennen van de eigenschappen van constructieve en hydraulische systemen (zoals massa, elasticiteit, debieten), die essentieel zijn voor de modellering;

LD2: Het kunnen formuleren van eenvoudige bewegingsvergelijkingen vanuit de tweede wet van Newton, met gebruik van de verplaatsingenmethode.;

LD3: Analytisch kunnen afleiden van de oplossingen voor geformuleerde bewegingsvergelijkingen;

LD4: Het kunnen interpreteren van oplossingen in termen van de fundamentele dynamica aanduidingen, zoals: eigenfrequentie, eigenperiode, vrije en geforceerde bewegingen, resonantie, en steady-state beweging;

LD5: Het kunnen voorstellen van adequate ontwerpverbeteringen voor de bestudeerde dynamica systemen.

Na dit vak succesvol te hebben doorlopen, moet de student in staat zijn om relevante fysische processen kwantitatief te kunnen beschrijven, om de werking van enkele veel voorkomende waterbouwkundige constructies te kunnen analyseren en om via synthese van verworven kennis en inzicht tot een voorontwerp van waterbouwkundige werken te kunnen komen. Meer specifiek gaat het erom dat de student in staat is om:

voor stationaire situaties, behoudswetten met betrekking tot massa, impuls en energie te kunnen toepassen om de waterbeweging in (netwerken van) open en gesloten waterlopen, zowel kwalitatief als kwantitatief, globaal te beschrijven;

transport van sediment en de relatie tot morfologie, zowel kwalitatief als kwantitatief, globaal te beschrijven;

vanuit de beginselen van golftheorie, golfcondities te voorspellen op basis van wind en voortplanting over open water;

op het niveau van voorontwerp, ontwerpcondities voor waterbouwkundige constructies vast te stellen en van deze constructies de functionaliteit en stabiliteit kwantitatief te kunnen evalueren;

de waterbeweging en energiehuishouding bij gemalen en waterkrachtcentrales, zowel kwalitatief als kwantitatief, globaal te beschrijven;

bij de analyse van fysische processen, evaluatie van constructies en toepassing van methoden die in het vak aan bod komen, de betrouwbaarheid en relevantie van uitkomsten te kunnen duiden.

description:
Samenvatting

Kennis van de hydrologische cyclus met bijbehorende fysische processen; deze in het veld kunnen herkennen en kunnen kwantificeren.

Onderdelen

Nut en doel van hydrologische kennis

Theorie over beschrijving van hydrologische processen

Hydrologische metingen

Hydrologisch modelleren

Ethische aspecten

Vakinhoud

De rol van hydrologie in mondiale watervraagstukken zoals overstromingen, droogtes, voedselvoorziening, drinkwater, energie, klimaatadaptatie;

Hydrologie van Nederland (geologie; neerslag en verdamping; oppervlaktewater; grondwater);

De kringloop van het water (componenten en processen; waterbalansen; reservoirs; systeembenadering; modellen);

Neerslag (het neerslagproces; neerslagtypen; fundamentele begrippen m.b.t. de neerslag; neerslagmeting; bewerking van neerslaggegevens; gebiedsgrootte-effect; neerslagnetwerken; neerslagvoorspellingen);

Verdamping (interactie tussen landoppervlak en atmosfeer; praktische betekenis van verdamping; verdampingstypen; standaard verdampingswaarden; fysica van de verdamping; factoren die invloed uitoefenen op de verdamping; energiebalans; berekening van de open-water verdamping; berekening van potentiële en referentieverdamping; verdampingsmetingen; waterverlies t.g.v. verdamping);

Grondwater (inleiding; waterstroming in poreuze media; schematisatie van de bodem; massabalans van een grondvolume in de verzadigde zone; massabalans van een grondvolume met freatisch vlak; zoet en zout grondwater, grondwater - onverzadigde zone, bodemenergie);

Afvoerhydrologie basisbeginselen (neerslag-afvoerprocessen; rivierafvoeren; metingen en afvoer-waterstandsrelaties; voorspellingen van kans op voorkomen op lange termijn middels extreme waarden van neerslag en afvoer; herhalingstijd; basisafvoer en uitputtingsverloop; voorspelling voor extreme gebeurtenissen op korte termijn middels het bergingsbeginsel (neerslagverlies, afvoerbare neerslag en stormafvoer)).

Stedelijke hydrologie en waterbeheer (stedelijke waterbalansen en afvoermodellen; oplossingen voor berging en afvoer in stedelijk gebied; looptijdbeginsel (rationele methode)).

learning_objectives:
Tijdens het BSc eindwerk werken studenten aan specifieke leerdoelen voor het BSc eindwerk en voor de ethiek leerlijn.

Leerdoelen BSc eindwerk

De student is in staat om:

Op basis van een ontwerp- of onderzoeksopdracht een probleemanalyse (inclusief beschrijving van projectimpact) te maken die leidt tot een doelstelling of vraag, en deze uit te werken naar een beredeneerde, onderbouwde en systematische aanpak en deze stapsgewijs uit te voeren;

Bij de aanpak passende methodes, technieken en tools te selecteren uit de bachelorvakken en deels uit aangereikte en zelf op te zoeken literatuur; om deze keuzes te onderbouwen met genoemde literatuur, gemotiveerde aannames en/of redenaties; en om deze technieken correct toe te passen;

(tussen)resultaten in tekst, figuren en tabellen samen te vatten, en de resultaten te interpreteren en hierbij de aannames en beperkingen van methoden, technieken en tools te betrekken;

Aan de hand van de bereikte resultaten te evalueren in hoeverre het doel (beantwoorden onderzoeksvraag, ontwerpdoel) behaald is en welke vragen opgeroepen zijn, de beperkingen van de conclusie te beschrijven en nieuwe vraagstukken aan te geven;

Suggesties voor vervolgwerk te doen (vervolgonderzoek, gedetailleerdere ontwerpen of verbeteringen), onder andere op basis van beschreven beperkingen en nieuwe vraagstukken;

De aanpak te plannen en deze zelfstandig uit te voeren en indien nodig de scope aan te passen;

Effectief en tijdig te communiceren over de voortgang van het project met de begeleider;

Kritische vragen te stellen over werk van medestudenten, opbouwende feedback te geven aan medestudenten, ontvangen feedback van begeleiders en medestudenten kritisch en proactief te verwerken;

Schriftelijk te rapporteren op een overzichtelijke, kernachtige en begrijpelijke manier zodat het werk reproduceerbaar is door anderen;

Een presentatie te geven over de essentie van het gedane werk aan medestudenten op een overzichtelijke, begrijpelijke en aansprekende manier, en om aan het project gerelateerde vragen te beantwoorden.

Leerdoelen ethiek leerlijn

Na het afronden van het BSc programma met alle bijhorende ethiek componenten is de student in staat om:

Sociale en ethische problemen in engineering en technologie te erkennen;

De professionele verantwoordelijkheden van ingenieurs en studenten in het licht van theorieën over verantwoordelijkheid te analyseren en op een valide manier toe te passen;

De morele impact van ingenieurswerk te analyseren met betrekking tot feiten, waarden, stakeholders, en stakeholder interesses;

Nieuwe opties om te acteren in het licht van (conflicterende) morele waarden en relevante feiten te ontdekken en te creëren;

Een besluit in een professionele context in het licht van een reflectie over verschillende ethische theorieën en frameworks te bereiken;

De redenen voor hun besluiten en acties te bediscussiëren, te evalueren en te rechtvaardigen en met andere ingenieurs en niet-ingenieurs.

het maken van een conceptueel ontwerp van gangbare waterbouwkundige kunstwerken;

voldoende gedetailleerd een haalbare bouwmethode beschrijven voor de constructie die wordt ontworpen;

het maken van de nodige schetsen of tekeningen, 2D of 3D, voor een conceptueel ontwerp;

de vereiste handmatige berekeningen uitvoeren voor het conceptueel ontwerp en aangeven voor welke onderdelen meer geavanceerde rekenmiddelen moeten worden gebruikt

description:
Functional design of roads and railway-infrastructure;

Roads and railways cross sections, horizontal and vertical alignment, integration of the horizontal and vertical alignments;

Roads intersection design, roundabouts and turbo-roundabouts, interchanges, and discontinuities;

Design for pedestrians and cyclists;

Design of trams in urban areas;

Human factors and road systems design;

Using AutoCAD and Civil3D;

This course is jointly given with AESB3340.

In this course, students acquire a fundamental basis for one- and two-phase flow in porous media. This course is an essential basis for all tracks within the MSc Applied Earth Sciences as well as the MSc track in Geotechnical Engineering in the MSc Civil Engineering. This course is highly recommended for students interested in Hydraulic Engineering as well as Hydro-geology and Water Management.

The course will address the following topics:

basic principles for groundwater flow under saturated conditions (1D, 2D and 3D, both stationary as well as non-stationary flow);

(potential) energy and its relationship with pressure and head;

capillary pressure and unsaturated flow (2-phase flow in a porous medium filled with water and air);

concepts of water and air content, saturation, hydraulic conductivity, relative permeability, and residual saturation;

water retention curves (van Genuchten, Brooks-Corey, and Gardner equations);

the impact of flow on soil mechanics, highlighting the connection with the parallel course of soil mechanics.

learning_objectives:
At the end of this course you will be able to:

explain the origins of groundwater flow;

derive the governing equations of the groundwater flow with appropriate initial and boundary conditions for saturated soils and unsaturated soils;

solve laboratory and field steady-state and transient problems for saturated (1D, 2D) and unsaturated (1D) flow by programming a Python/Matlab script;

discuss the quality and the limitations of the model results.

Develop and execute a project plan;

Develop a design proposal; go through the full design cycle for a given case;

Realize the integration of the different engineering disciplines;

Elaborate specific parts or aspects;

Oral and written presentation of the design proposal.

description:
This course consists of two parts:

Part 1: Functions of multiple variables

Part 2: Differential equations

In Part 1, we extend the student's knowledge of calculus applied to functions depending on one variable to functions depending on two or more variables.

We focus on the operations of differentiation and integration applied to such functions.

In addition, the student's "mathematical toolbox" will be expanded with techniques for working with vectors, taking derivatives and performing integration.

Topics in Part 1 are among others

Vectors, inner product, cross product;

Equations of lines and planes;

Inverse trigonometric functions;

Differentiation and Integration techniques;

Functions of multiple variables;

Partial and directional derivative;

Extreme values of functions;

Multiple integrals;

Coordinate systems.

In Part 2, we focus on differential equations. These are needed during the modeling of physical phenomena in the study of the earth and climate.

We focus on solving some specific types of differential equations.

Topics in Part 2 are among others

Complex numbers;

Direction field;

First and second order differential equations;

Taylor polynomials.

learning_objectives:
After a successful completion of this course the student should be able to:

compute the dot and cross product of two vectors and use this in other computations;

set up parametric and cartesian equations for lines and planes in three-dimensional space;

determine the domain of a function of multiple variables and compute its partial derivatives and tangent planes;

determine the gradient of a function of multiple variables, describe the gradient geometrically, use the gradient to compute tangent planes and directional derivatives and solve optimization problems;

compute multiple integrals;

decide whether it is beneficial to use polar, cylindrical and spherical coordinates to compute double and triple integrals and perform these computations;

set up a mathematical model to describe engineering problems using differential equations (DEs);

interpret a direction field;

solve first order linear and separable DEs;

use DEs to model vibrating springs;

solve second order linear DEs with constant coefficients;

approximate solutions to DEs using Taylor polynomials.

After taking this course you will be able to:

solve systems of linear equations and make a priori statements about the number of solutions;  

perform matrix operations (addition, multiplication, inverse, determinant) and can determine the matrix of a linear transformation; 

determine whether a set of vectors is linearly dependent or linearly independent, and relate this to other concepts in linear algebra; 

determine dimension and bases of linear subspaces, in particular of the null space and column space of a matrix; 

find the (possibly complex) eigenspaces and eigenvalues of a given matrix; 

determine whether a matrix is diagonalisable or not, can diagonalise a matrix if possible and use this to analyse dynamical systems; 

find orthogonal decomposition of a vector with respect to a linear subspace.

After completing the course, the student is able to:

Circuit theory competences / skills:

Express and apply the basic concepts of electrical circuits;

Estimate, examine and evaluate the basic circuital quantities (current, voltage, charge, energy, power);

Identify, interpret and examine the basic circuit elements: independent sources, resistances, inductances, capacitances, op-amps (strictly as linear circuit elements) and dependent sources; account for them via their specific operational/mathematical models;

Express Ohm's law and Kirchhoff's theorems, and apply them for calculating currents and voltages in circuits;

Identify series and parallel circuits, and apply the needed relations for calculating currents and voltages in circuits;

Express and interpret the nodal and mesh methods, and apply them to circuit analysis;

Express and interpret special instruments for analysing circuits: source transformation, superposition, Thévenin/Norton equivalents, and apply them to circuit analysis;

Identify and interpret first-order electric circuits, and apply the suitable formalism for evaluating their transient behaviour;

Identify and interpret second-order electric circuits, and apply the suitable formalism for evaluating their transient behaviour.

CourseLab / skills:

Interpret electronic schematics;

Solder electronic components;

Use basic laboratory (testing) equipment: sources, function generators, multi-meters, and oscilloscopes.

By the end of this course, you should be able to:

Electrical energy conversion components part:

- Explain the power system based on AC and DC connections;

- Tell how electromagnetic principles are applied in energy conversion;

- Describe the functioning of momentum of inertia, pulley en belt and gearboxes.

- Calculate the steady state operation of system containing mechanical components.

- Explain the construction and operation principle of a DC machine;

- Explain the nameplate of a given machine;

- Apply steady state performance calculation of a DC machine

- Explain the common operation principle of AC machines

- Explain the construction and operation principle of an induction machine;

- Apply steady state performance calculation of an induction machine;

- Explain the construction and operation principle of a synchronous machine;

- Apply steady state performance calculation of a synchronous machine;

- Tell the conditions to connect a synchronous machine to the AC power grid;

- Tell the roles of AC machines in modern power grid.

- Explain how to use power semiconductors and passive components to convert power;

- Tell main DC-DC converter topology (buck, boost)

- Analyze DC-DC converters at steady state;

- Describe the operation principles of H-brdige inverter and rectifier

Power system part:

- Describe a power system structure

- Model a power system

- Describe operation principles during normal conditions

- Describe operation principles during disturbances

- Apply methods to analyze a power system

At the end of this course, students should be able to:

Define EE fields

Recognize critical challenges in the field

Identify key contributions of EE to society

Classify programming languages

Constructing python codes

Discuss and reflect on teamwork, ethics, academic integrity, study skills (math, time management)

And gain the following mathematical competences / skills:

Solve systems of linear equations using row reduction;

Evaluate a one- or two-sided limit of a function at a point or at infinity;

Interpret the continuity of a function;

Evaluate derivatives of elementary functions and sums, products, quotients and compositions of these functions;

Use the derivative to describe the qualitative behaviour of a function;

Find the linearization of a function;

Evaluate definite and indefinite integrals of elementary functions and of (piecewise) continuous functions;

Evaluate definite and indefinite integrals using integration by parts;

Perform arithmetics involving complex numbers;

Determine the polar and exponential form of a complex number;

Find complex solutions of polynomial expressions;

Interpret a direction field of a first order differential equation;

Solve a first or second order, linear, homogeneous or nonhomogeneous differential equation or initial-value problem with constant coefficients;

Describe the movement of a mass-spring system using a second order, linear differential equation infer correspondences with circuit theory.

Upon successful completion of IP-2 project, the student will be able to:

Apply a project-based approach to design an electronic system in a project team;

Methodologically design a technical solution to a relevant problem within given boundary conditions;

Integrate and apply the knowledge and skills acquired during the lecture classes of the first year, in a design project;

Actively apply academic skills in order to intentify and acquire missing information and knowledge, systematically analyze problems, motivate choices, critically interpret results;

Document own project-related work, write a well-organized technical report about it and present it.

Enhancing teamwork skills by identifying your core qualities and pitfalls, challenges and allergies during a group project, and exploring how team members can learn from each other’s core qualities.

Apply ethical skills by evaluating value conflicts between design requirements using ethical theories and approaches.

After successfully completing the course, the student will be able to:

* formulate various boundary value problems for the one-dimensional and two-dimensional Poisson equation;

* derive these boundary value problems from the Maxwell equations for the magnetic field by introducing the scalar and vector potential;

* apply the finite difference and finite element method to solve these boundary value problems on a scale of problems of increasing complexity;

* state properties of the finite difference and finite element method applied to Poisson boundary value problems;

* describe the implementation of the finite difference and the finite element method in software;

* give a physical interpretation of the numerical solution obtained for magnetic field problems;

After successful completion of the course, you:

1. Know, understand and can apply (3) the functionality, construction and application of active implantable biomedical microsystems (AIBM's) and their sub-systems and parts;

2. Know, understand and can apply (3) the fundamental principles and approaches for designing an implantable system;

3. Know, understand, can apply, analyse and can reason about (evaluate; 5) the design requirements, application-specific implementation challenges, and appropriate design choices of existing AIBM's, incl. cardiac pacemakers, cochlear implants and spinal cord stimulators;

4. Know and understand (2) the most important implantability, biocompatibility, reliability, safety and security issues of AIBM's;

5. Know and understand (2) the most important regulatory and clinical constraints of AIBM's

6. Can reason (5) about future developments of AIBM's, a.o. electroceuticals and bioelectronic medicine;

NB. The numbers correspond with Bloom's taxonomy.

After this course students have to be able

* to recognize and explain different mechanisms of electromagnetic waves (EMW) propagation/scattering and relations between them;

* to assess different mechanisms of EMW propagation/scattering in relation to characteristics of EMW and media;

* to estimate quantitatively the influence of the propagation and scattering mechanisms on trade-offs that have to be made when designing a wireless communication or sensing system.

* to analyze real-world scenarios and identify the involved propagation and scattering mechanisms and their relevant aspects;

The student will be able to

1. derive and use the spectral Green's function method for EM problems (diffraction, optics, evaluation of far-fields);

2. apply electromagnetic methods (physical optics, Fourier optics) for quasi-optical systems and analyze focal plane arrays;

3. apply the mathematical tools for describing the behavior of periodic arrays;

At the end of the course, you should be able to:

1. Understand the principles of energy generation, storage and consumption;

2. Make simple estimations/calculations of the potential of (renewable) energy sources in reference to humankind energy consumption using simple physical tools/concepts.

3. Describe the operation principle and structure of basic building blocks of a power electronics based system; describe hardware and control architecture for regulating basic power converters.

4. Identify the system topologies of power electronics based systems enabling renewable energy transition;

5. Analyse basic power electronics converters at steady state based on flux linkage and charge balance;

6. Calculate voltage/current stress of critical components during normal operation of basic power converters and tell the sizing conditions of components based on it;

7. Carry out simulations and simple experiments of basic power conversion systems, and evaluate the results;

On completion of this elective, the student is able to:

1. Formulate a research or design question, aligned with the clients demands, as well as produce and conduct a plan of work evidently converging to an answer to that question;

2. Integrate and apply academic knowledge together with engineering and design skills acquired in previously taken modules of the master programme and relevant previous education.

description:
This module is aimed to teach students the fundamentals of physics and chemistry in the urban atmospheric environment. They will learn to appreciate the inherent complexity of the urban environment, where due to complex building geometry the atmosphere is highly non-uniform and transient in space and time. By performing order-of-magnitude (scaling) analysis of various physical and chemical processes, the students will learn to simplify complexity into a limited number of key-processes and to describe the evolution of relevant physical/chemical quantities using the (simplified) governing equations.

On an introductory level, the student will learn how those quantities can be probed by simple measurement principles. They will also learn basic tools of numerical modelling/conceptualization to analyze the impact of novel engineering designs on urban atmospheric environments.

While both measurement and modelling techniques are introduced on a basic level here as to understand the key processes, an in-depth treatise of these techniques are introduced in the next module.

Urban Climate Physics

Link to learning objectives of the module:

SS: LO1, LO2, LO3, LO4, LO5, LO6 and LO8.

The module Fundamentals requires a basic introduction to urban microclimate. Here we introduce a range of physical concepts that are utilized in the remainder of the track modules. Apart from theory, some simple experiments are performed as an introduction to more rigorous explanation of experimental principles in later modules.

The central theme of Urban Climate Physics is the scientific analysis of the urban climate. We aim at understanding the dominant physical processes that occur in urban environments as well as to understand the interactions between a city and the overlying atmosphere and how it impacts our living environment. Special emphasis is given to quantification of thermodynamic processes, such as radiative, convective and conductive heat transport and to airflow in complex geometries.

The theoretical part connects with short practical's which illustrates the various physical principles through simple hands-on experiments. These practical's facilitate students to develop scientific intuition for the processes at hand and prepare them for the rest of the module (Modelling of Urban Flows, Urban en Indoor Air Quality, and Urban Extreme Weather).

The following topics will be addressed:

Introduction and basic concepts;

Research Methodologies;

Transport phenomena and urban air flows (part I);

Transport phenomena and urban air flows (part II);

Radiative processes;

The urban energy balance (including moisture effects);

Human thermal comfort in cities.

Atmospheric chemistry

Link to learning objectives of the module:

SS: LO1, LO2, LO3, LO4, LO5, LO6 and LO8.

In Atmospheric chemistry a range of fundamental concepts for understanding the key chemical processes that take place in the atmospheric environment will be introduced. These concepts will allow the students to identify, analyze, and explain certain atmospheric phenomena including physicochemical reactions, that will prove to be highly useful for the rest of the module. Apart from theory, simple experiments will be included during which the students will be able to get hands-on experience.

The following topics will be addressed:

Background, composition, and structure of the atmosphere

Concepts of atmospheric chemistry (thermodynamics, kinetics, lifetime, photochemistry, etc);

From stratospheric to tropospheric chemistry;

Chemistry in urban and rural environments;

Basic properties of atmospheric aerosol particles;

Processes involving atmospheric aerosols*;

Impacts of air pollution on human health, the environment and climate. Air Pollution Meteorology en Dispersion

Link to learning objectives of the module:

SS: LO1, LO2, LO3, LO5 and LO6.

In Air Pollution Meteorology en Dispersion the fundamental concepts related to turbulent diffusion and dispersion are introduced. The more advanced Urban en Indoor Air Quality, Integrated AEE Project will leverage on these concepts.

Air Pollution Meteorology en Dispersion provides an in-depth understanding of how various atmospheric processes impact air pollutant transport, especially in urban environments. First, a number of fundamental concepts related to diffusion and dispersion (e.g., turbulence mixing, plume rise, deposition) are introduced. Then, different types of pollutant sources (e.g., point, line, area) and associated mathematical equations for diffusion are derived from the first principles. More advanced approaches, based on statistical and similarity theories, are discussed next. As complementary to the theoretical discussions, a state-of-the-art dispersion modelling software is utilized for further understanding of real-life pollutant transport problems. Lastly, accidental release of hazardous materials in mega-cities and related emergency response management will be briefly touched upon.

The following topics will be addressed:

Introduction; Gaussian diffusion models;

Plume rise, settling, and deposition;

Statistical theories of puff and plume diffusion;

Similarity theories of turbulence and diffusion;

Modelling dispersion at city scale;

Impacts of highways and buildings on urban air quality;

Accidental releases and emergency response management.

Physics of Sound and Human Perception

Link to learning objectives of the module:

SS: LO2, LO3, LO5, LO6 and LO8.

In Physics of Sound and Human Perception the fundamental concepts related to physics of sound and its effect on people are introduced. The more advanced Noise Modelling en Mitigation, Integrated AEE Project will build on these concepts.

Firstly, the physics of sound are introduced with a combination of practical experimentation and mathematical analysis. Basic examples such as open and closed tubes, and moving sources are studied to provide a solid foundation of acoustical understanding in preparation for the rest of the module. Then, the way we hear and respond to sound, the way we quantify sounds, and the relationship between those measures and human responses to the sounds are introduced.

More specifically the following topics will be covered in a number of teaching sessions combining theory and (in-class) experiments related to sound and its perception:

Derivation of the one-dimensional (1D) wave equation for a fluid-filled pipe based on first principles and explanation of the assumptions necessary to do so;

Evaluation and analysis of free vibrations of a fluid-filled pipe: the physical phenomena of resonance and standing waves in a fluid-filled pipe;

Derivation of the three-dimensional wave equation based on first principles and explanation the assumptions necessary to do so;

Explanation of the physics of the wave equation in application to acoustic problems;

Explanation of the meaning of common terms in wave mechanics, such as frequency, wavelength, wavenumber, wave speed, diffraction, reflection, dispersion and give examples of how they apply to sound;

Acoustic sources;

Speed of sound in a fluid medium and dependence on its physical properties;

Demonstration (by means of a computer program where appropriate) of standing waves, interference fields, and other wave phenomena;

Parts of the outer, middle and inner ear and their role in hearing;

Hearing of an individual;

Analysis of sound measurements;

Conversion of narrowband data into third and octave band levels either with linear or A weighting.

This course will not be offered in 2025-2026. Too few students preregistered for the course by the registration deadline (April 23rd, 2025).

Introduction into industrial classification, industrial symbiosis, water footprint, BAT reference documents;

Water intensive industries, including mining, chemical, textile, steel, paper, food and drinks;

Water intensive processes, including steam production and use, cleaning in place, cooling;

Water and energy, including water-energy nexus, heat recovery;

Water treatment technologies, including biological and physicochemical technologies;

Corrosion, scaling and fouling: interactions, prevention and mitigation;

Safety, ethics and risk assessment;

Modelling of corrosion and scaling;

Water use and reuse in selected companies: analyzing real cases and proposing solutions.

In this module a large variety of water qualities will be considered, ranging from ultra-pure water (e.g. makeup water for steam boilers) to concentrated residual water (e.g. brines from membrane filtration).

learning_objectives:
Protection of public health is an important element of civil and environmental engineers. In this course, students get knowledge about how disease agents are transmitted via water systems and how this can be prevented. Students will be able to evaluate whether a water system is safe, design water systems that are safe, and

After the course the students will:

be able to critically evaluate the health risks associated with engineered water systems (ILO 4);

understand agents that cause disease and how they can be transmitted via (engineered) water systems (ILO 2 en 8);

identify the importance of barriers in water systems to control disease agents (ILO 3);

assess how to design/operate water systems to protect public health and prevent health risks to occur (ILO 12);

describe the potential health risks associated with water systems in a systematic, quantitative manner using Health Impact Assessment and Quantitative Microbial Risk Assessment (ILO 7);

work in a team to recommend solutions for safe water systems (ILO 11);

report to stakeholder on risk management options for a safe water system on the basis of a scientifically justified quantitative assessment (ILO 9).

