- Recognize, explain, and illustrate the basic regimes of qubit dynamics and the basic sources of noise and decoherence relevant for different qubits;

- Identify typical scenarios of free and driven qubit dynamics, assess the relevant sources of decoherence and noise, and determine relevant models for description and analysis of dynamics of single- and multi-qubit systems;

- Apply these relevant models and evaluate the possible influence of noise and decoherence on the qubit dynamics in different typical regimes;

- Choose appropriate models for describing different types of qubits, determine corresponding noise and decoherence models, and calculate the parameters of the qubit dynamics in the presence of noise and coupling to the environment;

- Explain the basics of the dynamical decoupling protocols and the physical processes underlying this method of decoherence suppression;

- Apply the knowledge about qubit control and decoherence suppression to diverse qubits, such as impurity centers, quantum dots, (partially) superconducting qubits, and other similar systems;

- Choose and identify the dynamical decoupling protocols, and evaluate efficiency of different dynamical decoupling protocols under different conditions.

description:
Approximate syllabus:

- quantum states, unitary operations, and measurements;

- universal gate sets;

- entanglement, Bell test;

- basic quantum communication protocols;

- basic quantum algorithmic techniques and basic quantum algorithms;

- principles of quantum error correction and basic quantum error correction protocols

learning_objectives:
At the end of the course, the student can:

1. name and explain basic quantum computing concepts and can differentiate them from their classical counterparts;

2. name and explain the different complexity classes, illustrate them by giving concrete examples, and compare the performance of classical and quantum algorithms based on their complexity;

3. outline properties of main quantum algorithms by clearly defining their input, output and computational complexity in contrast to corresponding classical complexity and by exemplifying some of their main applications

4. implement basic quantum and hybrid quantum-classical algorithms in at least one of the mainstream quantum programming languages and assess/interpret the results (with and w/o noise)

5. describe the limitations of real-world quantum computing

Knowledge, insight, judgment and skills

After completing this course, students will be able to:

use the command line on a Linux system, perform basic file operations and job management tasks, and automate tasks with shell scripts;

use Git to commit and integrate incremental code changes together with a lab partner, and resolve any code integration conflicts;

use a Gitlab server to exchange code changes, and use its issue tracker to provide and receive feedback on each other's code;

solve programming tasks in C++, compile the code using Make, debug compile-time, link-time and run-time problems;

create new C++ ROS packages, use common ROS development tools, and utilize the ROS API in their C++ programs;

investigate and integrate external libraries into their own ROS components, such as OpenCV or PCL;

add code comments and basic documentation to their code, find and interpret documentation of external libraries;

Transferable and interpersonal skills

After completing this course, students will be able to:

develop evaluative judgments on the quality of your own work and of the performance of others (including peers) by implementing and providing feedback;

collaborate and coordinate with lab partner on coding tasks.

After successfully completing this course, you will be able to

- model real-world control and decision-making problems under uncertainty and in dynamic environments using the framework of discrete-time Markov decision process;

- compute relevant technical concepts including hitting probabilities, expected hitting times, invariant distributions, and the long-run proportion of time spent in a given state;

- formalize the optimal decision-making problem by deriving the respective dynamic programming principle;

- use the dynamic programming algorithm to find useful properties of the desired solution and solve the optimal decision-making problem.

After the course the student should be able to:

1) analyse the structure of a system and identify main components, the services provided by each and carry out a Fault Modes and Effects Analysis;

2) analyse and model faults or cyber attacks that can occur in a given dynamical system;

3) design an algorithm for detecting, isolate and identify them;

4) design a policy for reconfiguring the control system in order to accommodate them.

5) analyze the vulnerability of an industrial control system to cyber attacks and design prevention and detection algorithms

After completion of the course, the student is able to:

Explain what socio-technical systems are and what designing in a socio-technical system entails;

Apply methods for conceptual design, especially identifying and formulating insights, problem statements, generating designs for socio-technical interventions and evaluating alternatives;

Explain what an institution is, what institutional design entails, and how the designing of institutions differs from designing technical structures;

Explain what a multi-actor process is, what process design entails, and how the designing of processes differs from designing technical and institutional structures;

Can reflect on the complexity of the case that was used to practice designing.

After taking this module the student should be able to:

1) have in-depth knowledge of a research theme that represents the state of the art in research related to socio-technical systems;

2) conduct an individual structured literature review and present the findings in an academic article;

3) identify issues of scientific integrity/misconduct and to critically assess its practical and intellectual consequences.

description:
Students select a master thesis subject that has societal relevance and addresses an academic knowledge gap based on a structured literature review in order. To address (part of) the knowledge gap, the student develops a structured, academic research proposal. This includes formulating a main research question, selecting an appropriate research approach and formulating sub questions, selecting research methods and/or modelling techniques and data analysis tools. The project proposal is finalized by a research flow diagram and a thesis project planning.

During the course, the student will read and analyze approx. 10 scientific articles and conducts a peer-review of partial draft chapters and the final research proposal of peers in the course.

Final deliverables:

A research proposal in a predefined format. In order to pass and get access to the Master Thesis Project, this deliverable needs to be graded as sufficient (>= 5.75). It will be graded according to a fixed list of criteria;

Peer reviews as formative assignments that will be assessed by peers;

All assignments during the course have to be handed in before the set deadlines, and have to comply with the assessment criteria.

learning_objectives:
The aim of the CoSEM master thesis project is to design solutions for large and complex contemporary socio-technical problems. This requires the consideration of technical, institutional, economic and social aspects.

CoSEM students employ methods for creatively designing technical solutions in organisations and assessing their impact. The solutions contain system engineering approaches as well as effective management strategies to deal with the technological complexity and stakeholders with widely diverging interests. Thesis projects take into account both public and business values and look at the physical system as well as the actor network, confronting not only technical challenges, but also management and ethical choices.

During and after the CoSEM thesis project a student should be able to:

- develop a relevant scientific contribution to a CoSEM-related subject;

- determine the societal relevance of the thesis topic to which the master thesis will contribute;

- apply the knowledge and skills learned during the CoSEM programme;

- analyze the complexities of the socio-technical system and its institutional and/or managerial aspects;

- develop an integrated research design including research approach and methods, tools and data requirements to design, evaluate or simulate an innovation in a well-delineated part of a complex socio-technical system;

- critically evaluate the consequences of an intervention within a complex socio-technical system;

- formulate informed recommendations and future research topics based on the project results;

- defend and justify the research outcomes;

- independently manage, set up, carry out, report and present a research project.

During the Master thesis project, the student needs to demonstrate a thorough command of the knowledge and skills learned during the study programme and to show they are able to apply them to the master thesis project. This entails knowledge, insight and skills on an academic level for:

1. independently setting up and carrying out a research project;

2. project planning and management;

3. cooperating and communicating with others;

4. documenting, reporting and making a presentation;

5. the specific graduation subject.

General criteria for graduation are:

- the work contains an analytical component

- the work is multidisciplinary in nature

- the work focuses on a technical domain or application

description:
This course contains logistical, supply chain and innovations in logistics and supply chain topics.

Logistics Topics:

The significance of the logistics system(s) of companies in supply chains;

Methods for analyzing logistics systems of companies;

The role of production in logistics systems of companies;

The role of inventories in logistics systems of companies;

The role of transport between logistics systems of companies;

Connecting purchasing and customer service within companies;

Value added logistics, outsourcing and third party logistics;

Performance measurement in logistics systems;

Logistics control and information systems;

Design of logistics networks and determining locations for distribution centers.

Supply Chain Topics:

Global trade lanes, industrial clusters and supply chains;

Strategy issues and the supply chain;

Organizational issues and supply chains;

Supply Chain Mapping, Bench-marking and the SCOR-reference model;

Physical and Virtual Value Systems (MRP-I, MRP-II, ERP, and APS);

Supply Chain Value Adding Processes;

Distributions and Supply Chain Resilience;

Sustainability (triple P) within the supply chain;

Lean and Agile Supply Chains;

International Collaboration (Supply Chain Partnerships en Alliances);

Supply Chain Analysis;

Supply Chain Design.

Innovations in Logistics and Supply Chain Management:

The Last-Mile of Supply Chains;

Multi-, and Omni Channel Supply Chains;

Synchromodal Logistics, Synchro Supply Chains and the Physical Internet;

Customs Processes and Trade Compliance;

Orchestration and the application of Cross Chain Control Centers (4C);

Service Logistics (Asset Management, Maintenance Management and spare parts logistics);

Greening the Supply Chain, Circularity and Closed-Loop Supply Chains;

Smart Supply Chains (Automation, the Internet of Things and AI-applications);

Supply Chain Finance (Strategic Profit Model, Product Portfolio's and Resilience);

Transparency in Supply Chains (Smart Contracts, and Block Chains Technology);

Alternative Organized Supply Chains (especially: Distributed Autonomous Organizations as part of Supply Chains).

learning_objectives:
After this course students are able to:

evaluate and analyse the performance of energy (electricity) markets;

identify cases of market failures and design remedial action to rectify these market failures;

estimate the LCOE and calculate the NPV and IRR of an electricity investment project;

contrast private and social costs of (renewable) electricity generation;

assess technological lock-in in energy generation; and

appraise the role of public policy in energy markets and in energy technology innovation.

Je hebt kennis van de heersende opvattingen en theorieën in het onderwijs, kan hun waarde voor de onderwijspraktijk omschrijven en ze in de eigen onderwijspraktijk toepassen.

Je kunt:

jouw visie op de pedagogische verantwoordelijkheid van leraar en school expliciteren en die met argumenten onderbouwen;

de ontwikkeling van jouw pedagogische professionaliteit in termen van: pedagogisch bewust zijn (het kunnen zien van het belang van een leerling in een specifieke context), pedagogisch legitimeren (het kunnen verwoorden en bespreken van de pedagogische idealen die ten grondslag liggen aan het onderwijzend handelen) en pedagogisch handelen (het vorm kunnen geven aan pedagogische idealen bij het maken van onderwijs) beschrijven;

op systematische wijze gegevens verzamelen en analyseren voor de beantwoording van een leervraag over het eigen pedagogisch en/of didactisch handelen in de praktijk;

jouw eigen functioneren en docentgedrag zien als onderdeel van de interactie tussen leraar en leerling(en) en daarop reflecteren aan de hand van gegevens over die interactie;

het functioneren van (groepen) leerlingen analyseren vanuit een sociale systeembenadering;

basiskennis van de ontwikkelingspsychologie in de fase van adolescentie en basiskennis van zorg voor leerlingen vertalen naar jouw lespraktijk;

in jouw professionele handelen rekenschap geven van de maatschappelijke verantwoordelijkheid van het onderwijs in het algemeen en ten aanzien van burgerschapsvorming in het bijzonder.

description:
